# mdsvex

mdsvex is a markdown preprocessor for [Svelte][svelte] components. Basically [MDX][mdx] for Svelte.

This preprocessor allows you to use Svelte components in your markdown, or markdown in your Svelte components.

mdsvex supports all Svelte syntax and its own flavour of markdown, which differs from CommonMark in a few places. See [Markdown](docs#markdown) for what is different and [limitations](docs#limitations) for what is missing.

You can do this:

```svx
<script>
	import Chart from '#lib/Chart.svelte';
</script>

# Here’s a chart

The chart is rendered inside our mdsvex document.

<Chart />
```

mdsvex has its own markdown parser, and [parse plugins](docs#parse_plugins) can change the tree it builds.

[Try it](/playground)

## Install it

Install it as a dev-dependency.

With `npm`:

```bash
npm i --save-dev mdsvex@next
```

With `pnpm`:

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev mdsvex@next
```

mdsvex needs Svelte 5 and Vite 6 or later.

## Use it

mdsvex is a Vite plugin. It turns markdown files into Svelte components, and the Svelte plugin compiles them like any other component.

### SvelteKit

SvelteKit 3 has no `svelte.config.js`, so mdsvex goes in `vite.config.js`, beside `sveltekit()`:

```js
// vite.config.js
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
	plugins: [mdsvex({ extensions: ['.svx', '.md'] }), sveltekit()]
});
```

Declare the extensions once, in `mdsvex()`. It registers them with SvelteKit, so you don't repeat them in `sveltekit({ extensions })`. The order of the two plugins doesn't matter.

Any markdown file is now a component, and a `+page.svx` is a route:

```
src/routes/
├── +page.svelte
└── blog/
    └── hello/
        └── +page.svx
```

Options that point at a file, such as [`templates`](docs#templates) and [`components`](docs#components), resolve the way an import from your project root would. That includes aliases, packages, relative paths and SvelteKit 3's `#lib` [subpath imports][subpath], which replace `$lib` (see the [SvelteKit 3 migration guide][kit3]):

```json
{
	"imports": {
		"#lib/*": "./src/lib/*"
	}
}
```

```js
mdsvex({
	templates: { default: '#lib/templates/Post.svelte' }
});
```

Imports inside a document work the same way, `import Chart from '#lib/Chart.svelte'`. Subpath imports need the file extension.

### Vite without SvelteKit

With `@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte` on its own, add the extensions to `svelte()` as well. mdsvex can't register them there. If one is missing, the build stops with an error that names it.

```js
// vite.config.js
import { svelte } from '@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte';
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
	plugins: [mdsvex(), svelte({ extensions: ['.svelte', '.svx'] })]
});
```

### Highlighting code

Fenced code is highlighted by default, with [twinkleplop][twinkleplop]. mdsvex adds no CSS, so install a theme and import it once, in your root layout:

```bash
npm i --save-dev @twinkleplop/theme-github
```

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+layout.svelte -->
<script>
	import '@twinkleplop/theme-github';

	let { children } = $props();
</script>

{@render children()}
```

Without a theme, code renders in one colour. The theme gives light colours, and dark ones under a `.dark` class. To follow the reader's system setting instead, see [themes](docs#themes).

### `compile`

`compile` turns one document into Svelte component source without Vite. It is synchronous and never touches the filesystem, so it also runs in a browser.

In a browser or worker, import it from `mdsvex/compile`. That entry holds only the compiler, so a bundler never sees the Vite plugin or its lazy imports of Vite and Node builtins.

```js
import { compile } from 'mdsvex';

const { code, metadata } = compile(`---
title: Hello
---

# {metadata.title}
`);
```

The result has:

- `code`, the Svelte 5 component source;
- `metadata`, the parsed frontmatter, or `undefined` when there is none;
- `template`, the name of the template wrapping the document, or `undefined`;
- `warnings`, or `undefined` when there are none.

`compile` resolves nothing. Templates, components and directives are given as the import specifier to write plus the names that module exports. Finding those names is what the Vite plugin does for you.

```js
compile(source, {
	templates: {
		default: { specifier: '/src/Post.svelte', components: ['h2', 'p'] }
	},
	components: [{ specifier: '/src/markdown.js', names: ['img'] }],
	directives: [{ specifier: '/src/directives.js', names: ['Callout'] }]
});
```

The output imports from those specifiers exactly as written.

`compile` doesn't highlight code unless you pass `highlight`, see [highlighting with `compile`](docs#highlighting-with-compile).

A directive with no component is an error. For a preview or an editor, pass `strict_directives: false` to render it as its children instead.

#### `compile_async`

A plugin that waits for something, a file or an endpoint, [defers](docs#deferred-work) that work, and `compile` throws a `PluginAsyncError` when one does. `compile_async` takes the same options, waits for that work, and gives the same result once it applied:

```js
import { compile_async } from 'mdsvex/compile';

const { code, data } = await compile_async(source, { plugins: [citations()] });
```

A document whose plugins defer nothing compiles exactly as it does with `compile`. A task that fails rejects with a `PluginTaskError` that names the plugin and the line of the node, with the task's error as its `cause`.

Given `read` and `resolve` options, `compile_async` reads the files fences show with `src=` itself, resolved from `filename`, so a host that is not Vite needs no `sources`. The result's `dependencies` then name those files and the module of the template, beside what plugins read, for the host to watch:

```js
const { code, dependencies } = await compile_async(source, {
	filename: '/site/post.svx',
	read: (path) => fs.readFile(path, 'utf8'),
	resolve: (specifier, from) => path.resolve(path.dirname(from), specifier)
});
```

What plugins load with [`ctx.cached`](docs#loading-files) is kept between calls for as long as you pass the same `plugins` array, which suits a build that runs once. A host that compiles again when files change passes a `CompilerSession` of its own as the third argument, and calls its `invalidate` with the paths that changed before compiling the documents whose `dependencies` list them:

```js
import { CompilerSession, compile_async } from 'mdsvex/compile';

const session = new CompilerSession();
const { code, dependencies } = await compile_async(source, options, session);

// later, when a watched file changes
session.invalidate([changed]);
await compile_async(source, options, session);
```

#### Compiling in steps

A host that is not Vite, such as a playground or another bundler, compiles in steps so it can fetch what a document needs between them. This is what the Vite plugin does:

```js
import { CompilerSession, compile_inputs } from 'mdsvex/compile';

const session = new CompilerSession();
const inputs = compile_inputs(options); // the options compile takes
const parsed = session.parse(source, inputs);

// 1. what the plugins deferred, undefined when there is nothing to wait for
await session.settle(parsed);

// 2. what the document needs: the files its fences show with src=, and images
for (const need of parsed.needs ?? []) {
	if (need.type === 'source') sources[need.spec] = await read(need.spec);
}
inputs.sources = sources;

// 3. the render, with the same result compile gives
const { code, data } = session.render(parsed, inputs);
```

- **`parse`** runs the parser and the plugins. The `Parsed` it returns holds the document, its `metadata`, the `data` its plugins wrote so far and `needs`.
- **`settle`** waits for deferred work and applies it, and returns `undefined` when there was none, so a document without any stays synchronous. A document whose plugins deferred work throws if it renders before.
- **`render`** gives what `compile` gives. A `Parsed` renders once.
- **The session compiles other documents while one waits.** Each `Parsed` holds its own document.
- **`parsed.dependencies`**, and `dependencies` on the result, are the absolute paths its plugins read or named, for the host to watch. When one changes, call `session.invalidate([path])` before compiling the documents that depend on it again, so what `ctx.cached` kept from it goes.
- **`read` and `resolve` options** give plugins `ctx.read` and `ctx.resolve`.

## Markdown

mdsvex reads its own flavour of markdown, [Penguin-Flavoured Markdown][pfm]. It is CommonMark with the ambiguous parts taken out, the GFM additions most people use, and a few of its own. Most of a document reads as it does anywhere else. This section covers the rest.

Coming from 0.x, the [migrator](docs#run-the-migrator) rewrites your documents for you.

### Different from CommonMark

| you write | in CommonMark | in mdsvex |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `_text_` | emphasis | emphasis |
| `*text*` | emphasis | strong |
| `**text**`, `__text__` | strong | strong inside strong, and emphasis inside emphasis. Write `*text*` |
| a line of `===` or `---` under a title | a heading | a paragraph, and `---` is a thematic break. Write `# Title` |
| `## Title ##` | the closing `#` are dropped | they are text |
| four spaces of indent | a code block | nothing, indentation means nothing. Use a fence |
| `~~~` | a code fence | text. A fence is three or more backticks |
| two spaces at the end of a line | a line break | nothing. End the line with a backslash |
| a line without `>` after a quoted line | still in the quote | the quote has ended |
| `[text]` with a definition somewhere | a link | text. Write `[text][]`, with the definition above it |
| `https://example.com`, as GFM links it | a link | text. Write `<https://example.com>` |
| `{` and `}` | text | a Svelte expression. Write `\{` for a brace |

A link reference definition comes before the first link that uses it. That lets a document render while it is still arriving, since nothing later in it can change what came before.

```svx
[svelte]: https://svelte.dev

Made with [svelte][], see [the site][svelte].
```

A `_` or `*` opens after a space or punctuation and closes before one, so `snake_case_name` and `2*3*4` are text. To mark part of a word, put a `|` on the side that touches a letter. The `|` is not rendered:

```svx
fan|_tas_|tic
```

```html
<p>fan<em>tas</em>tic</p>
```

Curly quotes, dashes and other Unicode punctuation count as punctuation there. Chinese, Japanese and Korean text needs no marker, `这是*重要*的` is strong.

### Added to CommonMark

| you write | you get |
| --- | --- |
| `H~2~O` | subscript, H<sub>2</sub>O |
| `x^2^` | superscript, x<sup>2</sup> |
| `~~gone~~` | strikethrough |
| `- [x] done` | a [task list](docs#task-lists) |
| a pipe table | a [table](docs#tables), with header columns and merged cells |
| `:name[text]`, `::name`, `:::name` | a [directive](docs#directives), your own component |
| a `math` fence, `` $`x^2`$ `` | [math](docs#math-1) |
| `[^1]` in the text and `[^1]: a note` | a [footnote](docs#footnotes-1) |
| `import` lines at the top | [imports](docs#svelte-in-markdown) without a `<script>` |

### Task lists

A list item that starts with `[ ]` or `[x]` and a space is a task:

```svx
- [x] write the docs
- [ ] ship it
```

```html
<ul>
	<li><input type="checkbox" checked disabled /> write the docs</li>
	<li><input type="checkbox" disabled /> ship it</li>
</ul>
```

It works in bulleted and numbered lists at any depth. The marker needs text after it on its line, so `[x]done` and a bare `- [ ]` are text, and so is a marker anywhere but the start of an item. Write `- \[x] text` to start an item with those characters. A [replaced `li`](docs#element-props) gets `checked` and no checkbox.

### Tables

Tables are GFM pipe tables. A `:` in the delimiter row aligns a column, and `\|` is a bar inside a cell, in a code span too.

A `||` in the header and delimiter rows makes the columns on one side of it row headers:

```svx
| option || type | default |
| --- || --- | --- |
| `extensions` || `string[]` | `['.svx']` |
```

```html
<tr>
	<th scope="row"><code>extensions</code></th>
	<td><code>string[]</code></td>
	<td><code>['.svx']</code></td>
</tr>
```

A cell that holds only `>` joins the cell to its left, and one that holds only `^` joins the cell above:

```svx
| a | > | c |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 2 | 3 |
| ^ | 4 | 5 |
```

```html
<thead>
	<tr>
		<th colspan="2">a</th>
		<th>c</th>
	</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
	<tr>
		<td rowspan="2">1</td>
		<td>2</td>
		<td>3</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>4</td>
		<td>5</td>
	</tr>
</tbody>
```

Write `\>` or `\^` for a cell of that one character. A row is one line, and a table ends at a blank line or at any line that would end a paragraph, such as a heading.

### Svelte in markdown

Components, elements, expressions and blocks go anywhere, and markdown works inside them with no blank lines around it:

```svx
<Box>
# A title
</Box>

{#if published}
Some *strong* text.
{/if}
```

```svelte
<Box><h1>A title</h1></Box>{#if published}
<p>Some <strong>strong</strong> text.</p>{/if}
```

A block quote, a list item, an element, a Svelte block and a container directive all hold the same blocks the document does, so a table or a `{#if}` is the same thing inside a list item as at the top.

- **A paragraph of only tags gets no `<p>`.** `<Chart />` on a line of its own is the component and nothing else, and `<Badge /> new today` is a paragraph.
- **A block level element that starts a line is a block.** `<div>one</div> two` is the `<div>` and then a paragraph, `two`.
- **`<script>`, `<style>`, `<pre>` and `<textarea>` hold text.** Nothing inside them is markdown.
- **Imports can stand at the top**, after any frontmatter, without a `<script>`. They go into the document's script:

```svx
import Chart from './Chart.svelte'

<Chart />
```

```svelte
<script>
import Chart from './Chart.svelte'
</script><Chart />
```

- **A brace is an expression.** For a brace as text, write `\{`, use `&#123;`, or put it in code, where a brace is always text.

## Options

`mdsvex()` takes one object of options. They apply to every document.

```ts
interface MdsvexOptions {
	extensions?: string[];
	frontmatter?: {
		parse?: (raw: string) => Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined;
	};
	templates?: Record<
		string,
		string | URL | { component: string | URL; components?: string | URL }
	>;
	select_template?: (
		id: string,
		metadata: Record<string, unknown>
	) => string | false | undefined;
	components?: string | URL | (string | URL)[];
	component_mode?: 'markdown' | 'all';
	plugins?: Bundle[];
	parse_plugins?: ParsePlugin[];
	highlight?: HighlightOptions | Highlighter | false;
	math?: MathOptions | MathRenderer | false;
	footnotes?: { label?: string; back_label?: string; strict?: boolean };
	unwrap_images?: boolean;
	import_images?: boolean;
	links?: {
		routes?: string;
		base?: true | string | { import: string; from: string };
	};
	rewrite?: (
		url: string,
		ctx: { filename?: string; type: 'link' | 'image'; written: string }
	) => string | null | undefined | void;
	heading_ids?:
		| boolean
		| { slug?: (text: string, heading: { depth: number }) => string };
	smart_punctuation?:
		| boolean
		| {
				quotes?: boolean | [string, string, string, string];
				dashes?: boolean;
				ellipses?: boolean;
		  };
}
```

### `extensions`

```ts
extensions: string[] = ['.svx'];
```

The file extensions mdsvex compiles. To use `.md` files as components too:

```js
mdsvex({ extensions: ['.svx', '.md'] });
```

SvelteKit picks them up from here, see [SvelteKit](docs#sveltekit).

### `frontmatter`

```ts
frontmatter: { parse?: (raw: string) => Record<string, unknown> | null | undefined };
```

Replaces the built-in YAML parser. See [custom frontmatter parsers](docs#custom-frontmatter-parsers).

### `templates`

```ts
templates: Record<string, string | URL | { component: string | URL; components?: string | URL }>;
```

Components that wrap documents, by name. The `default` entry wraps every document that doesn't pick another. See [Templates](docs#templates-1).

### `select_template`

```ts
select_template: (id: string, metadata: Record<string, unknown>) => string | false | undefined;
```

Picks the template of a document whose frontmatter doesn't. See [choosing a template](docs#choosing-a-template).

### `components`

```ts
components: string | URL | (string | URL)[];
```

One or more modules whose exports replace elements in every document, below any template's own replacements. See [Custom components](docs#custom-components).

### `component_mode`

```ts
component_mode: 'markdown' | 'all' = 'markdown';
```

Which elements can be replaced. `'markdown'` only replaces elements written as markdown, `'all'` also replaces HTML you type. See [`component_mode: 'all'`](docs#component_mode-all).

### `plugins`

```ts
plugins: Bundle[];
```

A plugin brings directives: the components that render them and the handlers that reshape what is written inside them. A package exports a function that returns one, and you call it:

```js
import { steps } from '@acme/steps';

mdsvex({ plugins: [steps()] });
```

````svx
:::steps

## Install

```sh
pnpm add mdsvex
```

## Configure

Add it to the config.
:::
````

Here each heading inside `:::steps` starts a step. The plugin wraps the heading and what follows it in a `step` directive with the heading as its label, so the page renders the plugin's `steps` and `step` components.

The first-party plugins are listed under [Plugins](docs#plugins-1).

A plugin is an object:

```js
export function steps() {
	return {
		name: 'steps',
		// exports a component for each directive below, under its name
		module: '@acme/steps/directives',
		directives: {
			steps: {
				children: {
					heading(node, ctx) {
						ctx.state.step?.close();
						ctx.state.step = node.wrap_from('directive_container', { name: 'step' });
						node.wrap_from('directive_label').close();
					}
				}
			},
			step: {}
		}
	};
}
```

| key | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `name` | names the plugin in errors and in the editor manifest |
| `module` | the module that exports the directive components. It resolves as an import from the Vite root would |
| `directives` | one entry per directive name the plugin owns |
| `parse` | a [parse plugin](docs#parse_plugins) for the whole document, the same object an entry of `parse_plugins` is |
| `components` | element replacements, as `{ specifier, names }` |

An entry of `directives` can hold handlers. They are bound to that directive, so they never run anywhere else in the document:

| key | called for |
| --- | --- |
| `children` | nodes written directly inside a container directive of this name, by node type |
| `descendants` | nodes at any depth inside it, by node type |
| `parse` | the directive itself, in any of its three forms |

Each handler gets the node and a context:

- `ctx.directive` is the directive it is bound to.
- `ctx.index` counts the calls of this handler in this directive, from 0.
- `ctx.state` is an empty object, new for each directive in the document. Keep what a handler needs between calls there, such as the step it opened last.

A handler can return a function, which runs when the node closes. The directive's own `parse` can read its args when it opens and write them when it closes:

```js
tabs: {
	parse(tabs, ctx) {
		return () => {
			tabs.attrs.args = { ...tabs.attrs.args, count: String(ctx.state.count ?? 0) };
		};
	},
	children: {
		heading(node, ctx) {
			ctx.state.count = ctx.index + 1;
		}
	}
}
```

Args are always strings, since they reach the component as string props.

Things to know:

- **A nested blockquote or list is not a child.** `children` means written directly inside the directive. Use `descendants` for every node of a type at any depth.
- **Only containers have children.** The bracket text of a leaf or inline directive is not passed to `children` or `descendants` handlers. Their own `parse` still runs, though an inline directive gets its args after it, so read them in the function `parse` returns.
- **Your own export wins.** A `directives` export of the same name from a [`components` module](docs#declaring-directives) or a template replaces the plugin's component. The plugin's handlers still run, so your `step` gets the same `label` and `children`.
- **A later plugin wins a name.** When two plugins have an entry for the same directive, the later one's component and handlers are used.
- **Other plugins still see the nodes.** A heading inside `:::steps` reaches the `heading` handlers of `parse_plugins` too, after the bound handler ran. A plugin's `parse` runs ahead of `parse_plugins`.

`compile()` takes `plugins` as well. It writes `module` as the import specifier exactly as given.

What a handler gets and can do is under [plugin handlers](docs#plugin-handlers).

### `parse_plugins`

```ts
parse_plugins: ParsePlugin[];
```

Parse plugins change the tree as the parser builds it. A plugin is an object keyed by node type. Each `parse` function gets the node, and can return a function that runs when the node closes, once everything inside it is known:

```js
const external_links = {
	link: {
		parse(node) {
			return () => {
				if (/^https?:/.test(node.href)) node.attrs.rel = 'noopener';
			};
		}
	}
};

mdsvex({ parse_plugins: [external_links] });
```

A plugin with `sequential: true` runs over the finished tree instead, after [`heading_ids`](docs#heading_ids) has set its ids.

A sequential plugin can also rewrite text. Its `text` function is called for each run of text in the document, and `run.replace` puts text or a node in place of a range of it:

```js
const issues = {
	sequential: true,
	text(run) {
		for (const match of run.text.matchAll(/#(\d+)/g)) {
			const end = match.index + match[0].length;
			run
				.replace(match.index, end, 'link', { href: `/issues/${match[1]}` })
				.append('text', match[0]);
		}
	}
};
```

A run is whole however the tree split the text, and the text around a replacement stays as you wrote it. Code, math, urls, attribute values and `{expressions}` are never in a run, and a character you escaped with a backslash is left out of it.

Elements a plugin creates count as markdown, so a [replacement](docs#custom-components) for `mark` also replaces the `<mark>` a highlight plugin adds.

`parse_plugins` is the short way to add a few handlers from your config. To ship them with directives and components, or under a name, put the same object in the `parse` key of a [plugin](docs#plugins).

### Plugin handlers

Every handler, in `parse_plugins` or in a [plugin](docs#plugins), gets a node and a context. Handlers are keyed by node type:

`paragraph`, `heading`, `emphasis`, `strong_emphasis`, `strikethrough`, `superscript`, `subscript`, `code_span`, `code_fence`, `link`, `image`, `block_quote`, `list`, `list_item`, `table`, `table_header`, `table_row`, `table_cell`, `thematic_break`, `hard_break`, `soft_break`, `html`, `html_comment`, `mustache`, `svelte_tag`, `svelte_block`, `svelte_branch`, `directive_inline`, `directive_leaf`, `directive_container`, `directive_label`, `frontmatter` and `import_statement`.

An element or a component is an `html` node, with its name in `attrs.tag` and what was written on it in `attrs.attributes` once it has closed. An expression is a `mustache`.

The node is a view of the tree as it is being built:

| member | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `type` | the node type. Writing it changes what the node renders as |
| `attrs` | its attributes. Writing one sets it on the element, or passes it to a [replacement](docs#custom-components) as a prop |
| `text_content` | the text inside it |
| `parent`, `first_child`, `last_child`, `next`, `prev` | its neighbours, each a node or `null` |
| `depth`, `href`, `title`, `info`, `math`, `ordered`, `start`, `tight` | what its type has: the depth of a heading, the url of a link, the info string of a fence |
| `position` | `{ start, end }`, each with an `offset`, a `line` and a `column`. `end` is not set until the node closes |
| `pending` | `true` while the parser may still take the node back |
| `prepend(type, attrs)`, `append(type, attrs)` | adds a child and returns it. For `'text'` the second argument is the text |
| `wrap_inner(type, attrs)` | puts a new node between the node and its children |
| `wrap_from(type, attrs)` | puts a new node around the node and every sibling after it, until you call `close()` on what it returns |
| `remove()` | takes the node out of the tree |

A new `html` node takes its element name as `tag`. This wraps each `##` heading and what follows it in a `<section>`:

```js
const sections = {
	heading: {
		parse(node, ctx) {
			if (node.depth !== 2 || node.parent?.type !== 'root') return;
			ctx.section?.close();
			ctx.section = node.wrap_from('html', { tag: 'section' });
		}
	}
};

mdsvex({ parse_plugins: [sections] });
```

```svx
## One

First.

## Two

Second.
```

```html
<section><h2>One</h2><p>First.</p></section>
<section><h2>Two</h2><p>Second.</p></section>
```

The context is new for each document, and any key you set on it is yours, as `ctx.section` is above. It also has:

| member | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `ctx.filename` | the path of the document |
| `ctx.frontmatter` | the parsed frontmatter, `undefined` when the document has none. Don't write to it |
| `ctx.warn(code, message, node)` | reports a problem. The build prints it with the line of the node |
| `ctx.set(key, value)`, `ctx.add(key, value)` | write what the plugin leaves for the page, see [plugin data](docs#plugin-data). They need a `name` |
| `ctx.data` | what the plugin has written so far, read-only |

```js
const one_title = {
	name: 'one_title',
	parse: {
		heading: {
			parse(node, ctx) {
				if (node.depth === 1 && ctx.frontmatter?.title !== undefined) {
					ctx.warn('two_titles', `${ctx.filename} has a title and a # heading`, node);
				}
			}
		}
	}
};

mdsvex({ plugins: [one_title] });
```

A handler [bound to a directive](docs#plugins) has the same members beside `directive`, `index` and `state`. Its `children` table also takes `'*'`, a handler for a child of any type, which runs after the one for its type.

Things to know:

- **Handlers run while the document is read.** The parser opens some nodes on a guess, such as an emphasis before it has seen the closing `_` or a link before its url. When the guess is wrong it takes the node back, and everything a handler did for that node is undone. `node.pending` is `true` for such a node.
- **`wrap_from` needs a node that is not pending.** Headings and block directives never are. For a link or an emphasis, call it from the function the handler returns, which runs once the node has closed.
- **`remove()` works where nothing can ask for the node back**: in the function a handler returns, for a node that is not pending, or anywhere in a `sequential` plugin. A node removes itself or something inside it.

```js
// comments do not reach the page
const no_comments = {
	html_comment: {
		parse: (node) => () => node.remove()
	}
};
```

- **Handlers are synchronous.** For work that waits, see [deferred work](docs#deferred-work) below. A plugin can't add syntax either, only change the tree the parser made.
- **A plugin sees the text as written.** [`smart_punctuation`](docs#smart_punctuation) is applied when the page renders, after every handler.

[`PLUGINS.md`][plugins-md] in the repository has the whole API, with what a revoke undoes and how handlers run over a stream.

### Deferred work

A handler can't wait for a file or a fetch. It starts that work with `ctx.defer(node, task)` and the parse goes on:

```js
const titles = {
	name: 'titles',
	parse: {
		link: {
			parse(node, ctx) {
				return () =>
					ctx.defer(node, async (node, ctx) => {
						const page = await fetch(node.attrs.href).then((r) => r.text());
						node.attrs.title = page.match(/<title>(.*?)<\/title>/)?.[1];
						ctx.add('links', node.attrs.href);
					});
			}
		}
	}
};
```

- **The task gets a node and a `ctx` of its own** and changes the document through them. Its changes apply once it has finished and the parser is sure of the node. If the parser takes the node back, they are dropped.
- **A task changes its own node** and what is inside it, and writes data for it. Anything else throws a `TypeError`. So the result does not depend on which task finishes first.
- **The Vite plugin waits** for deferred work before it renders the document, and so does a `?data` import. With `compile`, use [`compile_async`](docs#compile_async). In dev it warns when a document has waited five seconds, naming the plugin.

Work that needs the whole document goes in a `document` hook. It runs once, after the parse and after every deferred task, and gets the root of the tree. It may be async:

```js
const summary = {
	name: 'summary',
	parse: {
		async document(root, ctx) {
			let headings = 0;
			for (let child = root.first_child; child !== null; child = child.next)
				if (child.type === 'heading') headings++;
			ctx.set('headings', headings);
		}
	}
};
```

Hooks run in plugin order, each awaited before the next. One that returns a promise needs `compile_async`, as deferred work does.

#### Loading files

A plugin loads what it needs itself, and tells mdsvex what it read:

- **`ctx.depend(path)`** names a file the output depends on, by its absolute path. When it changes, the Vite plugin compiles the document again, and its `?data` import, in dev and in a watch build, and it watches files outside the project root too. `compile` returns them as `dependencies` for any other host to watch.
- **`ctx.read(path)`** reads a file and depends on it. **`ctx.resolve(specifier)`** resolves a specifier as an import from the document would. The Vite plugin supplies both. With `compile_async`, pass `read` and `resolve` options, `mdsvex/compile` reads no files itself.
- **`ctx.cached(key, load)`** shares one load between documents: a bibliography read once for every page. A key that is a path is a dependency, and the Vite plugin drops it when that file changes, so a dev server never serves a stale copy.

```js
ctx.defer(node, async (node, ctx) => {
	const path = await ctx.resolve(ctx.frontmatter.bibliography);
	const bib = await ctx.cached(path, (path) => ctx.read(path).then(parse_bib));
	node.attrs.label = bib.get(node.text_content)?.short;
});
```

### Plugin data

A plugin can hand something back from the parse of a document, such as a table of contents or a reading time. `ctx.set(key, value)` keeps one value under a key and `ctx.add(key, value)` adds an entry to a list. `ctx.data` reads what the plugin has written so far:

```js
const reading_time = {
	name: 'reading_time',
	parse: {
		sequential: true,
		paragraph: {
			parse(node, ctx) {
				const words = (ctx.data.words ?? 0) + node.text_content.split(/\s+/).length;
				ctx.set('words', words);
				ctx.set('minutes', Math.ceil(words / 200));
			}
		}
	}
};

mdsvex({ plugins: [reading_time] });
```

The document exports what its plugins left as `plugin_data`, beside `metadata`, keyed by plugin name:

```js
import Article, { metadata, plugin_data } from './article.svx';

plugin_data.reading_time; // { words: 420, minutes: 3 }
```

- **The document can read it too**, as `{plugin_data.reading_time.minutes}` in its own markup.
- **A [template](docs#template-props) gets it as its `plugin_data` prop.**
- **A document whose plugins left nothing has no such export**, and compiles exactly as it does without plugins. Import it from a document you know has some, or use `?data` below, which always has the export.
- **A write belongs to the node whose handler made it.** When the parser takes a node back, the writes its handlers made go too, so a handler can write as soon as it knows something.
- **Lists are in document order.** An entry from `ctx.add` sorts by where its node is in the document, whatever order the handlers ran in. Of two nodes that `ctx.set` one key, the later one in the document wins.
- **`ctx.data` is read-only.** Assigning to it throws a `TypeError`, and what it holds is frozen.
- **The values are JSON**: plain objects, arrays, strings, finite numbers, booleans and `null`. Anything else, such as a `Date`, a `Map`, `NaN` or a function, throws a `PluginDataError` from the write, with the plugin, the key, the path of the value, `headings[2].id`, and where the node is. A key that holds `undefined` is left out.

To list documents with what their plugins left, import them with the `?data` query:

```js
// src/routes/blog/+page.js
const posts = import.meta.glob('./*/+page.svx', { query: '?data', eager: true });

export function load() {
	return {
		posts: Object.entries(posts).map(([path, { metadata, plugin_data }]) => ({
			slug: path.split('/')[1],
			title: metadata?.title,
			minutes: plugin_data?.reading_time.minutes
		}))
	};
}
```

A `?data` import parses the document and runs its plugins, and renders nothing, so the list does not load the component of every page. Its module exports `metadata` and `plugin_data`, each `undefined` when the document has none, and both in one object as its default export.

**`?data` or [`?metadata`](docs#frontmatter)?** A `?metadata` import reads the frontmatter alone, so only an edit of the frontmatter reloads the pages that import it. A `?data` import depends on the whole document: an edit of the body that changes what a plugin left reloads them, and a plugin that throws fails the import. Use `?metadata` for a list of titles and dates, and `?data` when the list shows something a plugin worked out.

For TypeScript, a plugin types its own key by adding to the `PluginData` interface, and `mdsvex/globals` in `compilerOptions.types` types direct `?data` imports:

```ts
declare module 'mdsvex' {
	interface PluginData {
		reading_time: { words: number; minutes: number };
	}
}
```

### `highlight`

```ts
highlight: HighlightOptions | Highlighter | false = {};
```

How code is highlighted. Options configure twinkleplop, a function replaces it, and `false` renders plain code. See [Syntax highlighting](docs#syntax-highlighting).

### `math`

```ts
math: MathOptions | MathRenderer | false = {};
```

How a `math` fence and inline math render. `renderer` picks `'temml'`, the default, or `'katex'`, and `options` goes to that library. A function is a renderer of your own, and `false` leaves the fence a code block and inline math inline code. See [Math](docs#math-1).

### `footnotes`

```ts
footnotes: { label?: string; back_label?: string; strict?: boolean } = {};
```

The accessible names of the [footnotes](docs#footnotes-1) section and of its back links, for a site that is not in English. `strict: false` builds a document whose footnotes a compile rejects, with warnings in place of the error.

### `unwrap_images`

```ts
unwrap_images: boolean = false;
```

An image on a line of its own is a paragraph, so it renders as `<p><img /></p>`. That is a problem once an `img` [replacement](docs#custom-components) renders a block element, because a `<figure>` is not allowed inside a `<p>`:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/Figure.svelte -->
<script>
	let { src, alt, title } = $props();
</script>

<figure>
	<img {src} {alt} />
	{#if title}<figcaption>{title}</figcaption>{/if}
</figure>
```

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as img } from './Figure.svelte';
```

```js
mdsvex({ components: '#lib/markdown.js', unwrap_images: true });
```

With `unwrap_images`, a paragraph that holds only images gets no `<p>`, so the figure stands on its own:

```svx
Sales grew all year.

![A chart](/chart.png "Sales by month")
```

```html
<p>Sales grew all year.</p>
<figure>
	<img src="/chart.png" alt="A chart" />
	<figcaption>Sales by month</figcaption>
</figure>
```

Several images in one paragraph are all unwrapped, on one line or on lines of their own. So is an image inside a link, such as `[![A chart](/chart.png)](/full.png)`, where the link stays around the image. A paragraph with any text beside the image keeps its `<p>`, and so does one with a hard line break between two images. It works the same inside block quotes, list items, directives and HTML elements.

`compile()` takes the option too, and the editor reads it from the manifest the plugin writes, or from `unwrap_images` in an `mdsvex.config.json`.

### `import_images`

```ts
import_images: boolean = true;
```

A markdown image with a relative url is imported, and the image gets what the import gives. Vite then hashes the file, inlines a small one, and fails the build when the file is missing.

```svx
![A chart](./chart.png)
```

```svelte
<script>
	import __mdsvex_img_0 from './chart.png';
</script>

<p><img src={__mdsvex_img_0} alt="A chart" /></p>
```

The path is relative to the document, as it is on GitHub and in an editor preview. Which urls are imported:

| url | imported as |
| --- | --- |
| `./chart.png`, `../img/chart.png` | as written |
| `chart.png`, `img/chart.png` | `./chart.png`, `./img/chart.png` |
| `$lib/chart.png`, `#lib/chart.png`, `@/chart.png`, `~/chart.png` | as written, so Vite resolves the alias |
| `./chart.png?url`, `./sprite.svg#icon` | as written, the query and the fragment stay on the import |
| `./my%20chart.png` | `./my chart.png`, the path is decoded to name the file |
| `/chart.png` | not imported |
| `https://example.com/chart.png`, `//example.com/chart.png`, `data:…` | not imported |
| `#section`, `?query` | not imported |
| `{src}`, `./{name}.png` | not imported, a url with a Svelte expression is left to Svelte |

A file used twice is imported once. An alias has to start with `$`, `#`, `@` or `~`. Any other bare path is a file beside the document, so `images/chart.png` is `./images/chart.png` even when `images` is an alias in your Vite config.

A query reaches Vite, so `![A chart](./chart.png?url)` and `?inline` work as they do in any import. With a query that makes the import something other than a url, such as `?enhanced` from `@sveltejs/enhanced-img`, an `img` [replacement](docs#custom-components) decides what to do with the value. It gets the imported value as its `src` prop.

A file in `static` or `public` is served from the root, so write it as `/chart.png`. Only markdown images are imported. An `<img src="./chart.png">` you type as HTML, a link to an image, and a url in frontmatter stay as written.

Set `import_images: false` to leave every url as written:

```js
mdsvex({ import_images: false });
```

`compile()` takes the option too. It is off there by default, because the import only means something to a bundler that takes image files. The editor compiles without it, so a replaced `img` always sees a string `src` there.

### `links`

```ts
links: {
	routes?: string;
	base?: true | string | { import: string; from: string };
}
```

Turns the links you write between documents into the urls your site serves, with no function to write. Both keys are off until you set them.

`routes` is the directory whose files are served by their path, relative to the Vite root. A relative link to a document in it becomes that document's route, so a link works on GitHub and in an editor as well as on the site.

```js
mdsvex({ links: { routes: 'src/routes' } });
```

```svx
<!-- src/routes/guide/+page.svx -->
[Install](../setup/+page.svx#install) and then read [the rest](./advanced/+page.svx).
```

```html
<p><a href="/setup#install">Install</a> and then read <a href="/guide/advanced">the rest</a>.</p>
```

How a file becomes a route:

| link, from `src/routes/guide/+page.svx` | route |
| --- | --- |
| `./other.svx`, `other.svx` | `/guide/other`, the extension goes |
| `../setup/+page.svx` | `/setup`, a last `+page` or `index` goes |
| `../+page.svx`, `../index.svx` | `/` |
| `../(marketing)/pricing/+page.svx` | `/pricing`, a `(group)` goes |
| `./other.svx?tab=a#x` | `/guide/other?tab=a#x`, the query and the fragment stay |
| `../[slug]/+page.svx` | left as written, nothing says which page it is |
| `../../lib/notes.svx` | left as written, the file is outside `routes` |
| `./chart.png`, `./data.json` | left as written, only a file with one of your [`extensions`](docs#extensions) is a document |

`base` goes in front of every url that starts with one `/`, for links and for images, and after `routes` has made its routes. It takes one of three things:

```js
// the base of your vite config
mdsvex({ links: { base: true } });
// a prefix of your own
mdsvex({ links: { base: '/docs' } });
// a function the page calls where it renders, such as resolve from SvelteKit
mdsvex({ links: { base: { import: 'resolve', from: '$app/paths' } } });
```

```svx
[Guide](/guide) ![Logo](/logo.png)
```

```svelte
<!-- base: '/docs' -->
<p><a href="/docs/guide">Guide</a> <img src="/docs/logo.png" alt="Logo" /></p>

<!-- base: { import: 'resolve', from: '$app/paths' } -->
<script>
	import { resolve as __mdsvex_base } from '$app/paths';
</script>

<p><a href={__mdsvex_base('/guide')}>Guide</a> <img src={__mdsvex_base('/logo.png')} alt="Logo" /></p>
```

Use the function form in SvelteKit, where the base is only known when the page renders. Any module works: the export is called with the url and returns the url to use. `import: 'default'` names a default export. A document with no root url imports nothing. `base: true` does nothing when Vite's base is relative or `/`.

Neither key touches a url with a scheme (`https:`, `mailto:`), a `//host` url, a `#fragment` or `?query` alone, or a url with a Svelte expression such as `/posts/{slug}`. Only markdown links and images are changed. An `<a href>` or `<img src>` you type as HTML stays as written, and so does an image that [`import_images`](docs#import_images) imports.

`compile()` takes `links` too. There `routes` is compared with `filename` as written, so give both as absolute paths or both relative to one directory, `extensions` inside `links` says which files are documents (`.md` and `.svx` by default), and `base: true` is an error since there is no Vite config to read.

### `rewrite`

```ts
rewrite: (
	url: string,
	ctx: { filename?: string; type: 'link' | 'image'; written: string }
) => string | null | undefined | void;
```

A function for whatever [`links`](docs#links) does not cover. It is called for the url of every markdown link and of every markdown image that is not imported. A string replaces the url, and returning nothing keeps it.

```js
mdsvex({
	rewrite(url, { type }) {
		// [the repo](gh:pngwn/MDsveX)
		if (url.startsWith('gh:')) return 'https://github.com/' + url.slice(3);
		// a trailing slash on every internal link
		if (type === 'link' && /^\/(?!\/)[^?#]*[^/?#]$/.test(url)) return url + '/';
	}
});
```

`ctx.filename` is the document, `ctx.type` says whether the url is the `href` of a link or the `src` of an image, and `ctx.written` is the url as the document has it.

It runs after `links.routes` and before `links.base`. So `url` is already a route for a link to a document, and a root url you return still gets the base. It sees external urls and `#fragment` links too, since some sites rewrite those, so return nothing for a url you do not mean to change. It never sees a url with a Svelte expression, an empty url, or the `href` or `src` of an element typed as HTML. Returning `''` leaves the attribute out.

The url is the text of the document, with character references such as `&amp;` not decoded. What you return is written the same way, so returning `url` changes nothing. It is called once for each different url in a document, and it must be synchronous.

`compile()` takes the option too.

### `heading_ids`

```ts
heading_ids: boolean | { slug?: (text: string, heading: { depth: number }) => string } = false;
```

Gives every markdown heading an `id` made from its text, so a link can point at it:

```js
mdsvex({ heading_ids: true });
```

```svx
## Getting _started_

## Getting started

## The `highlight` option
```

```html
<h2 id="getting-started">Getting <em>started</em></h2>
<h2 id="getting-started-1">Getting started</h2>
<h2 id="the-highlight-option">The <code>highlight</code> option</h2>
```

The id is the one GitHub makes: the text in lowercase, punctuation dropped, each space a hyphen, and letters of any script kept. A second heading with the same text gets `-1`, a third `-2`.

The text is what a reader sees. Emphasis, links and code contribute their text without the markup, and an image contributes its alt text. What Svelte renders contributes nothing, because it isn't known until the page runs: an expression such as `{version}`, a component and a directive. A heading that is left with no text, such as `## {title}`, gets no `id`.

A heading that already has an `id` keeps it, whether a [parse plugin](docs#parse_plugins) set it or you typed the heading as HTML, and no id made after it repeats it. Headings typed as HTML are otherwise left alone.

Each heading gets its id as it closes, from the ids before it, and an id never changes once given. An id you write yourself, on a heading typed as HTML or through a plugin that reads one from your text, is kept, and one that an element before it already has fails the compile with a `HeadingIdError` that names both. Footnotes are numbered at the end, so a footnote whose id a heading took moves aside instead: `## Fn 1` keeps `fn-1` and the footnote `[^1]` becomes `fn-1-1`.

For another scheme, pass a `slug` function. It gets the text and the depth of the heading, and mdsvex numbers duplicates in what it returns. An empty string leaves the heading without an `id`. `heading_slug` is the default:

```js
import { mdsvex, heading_slug } from 'mdsvex';

mdsvex({
	heading_ids: {
		slug: (text, { depth }) => (depth === 1 ? 'top' : heading_slug(text))
	}
});
```

The id is there for the function a parse plugin returns from its handler, which runs when the heading closes, and for every sequential plugin, so one can add an anchor that points at its heading:

```js
const anchors = {
	heading: {
		parse(node) {
			return () => {
				node.prepend('link', { href: `#${node.attrs.id}`, 'aria-hidden': 'true' });
			};
		}
	}
};

mdsvex({ heading_ids: true, parse_plugins: [anchors] });
```

[`@mdsvex/plugin-anchors`](docs#anchors) is that plugin, with a choice of where the link goes.

A [replacement](docs#custom-components) for a heading gets the `id` as a prop. `compile()` takes the option too. A streaming render makes the same ids as the heading arrives: `make_heading_ids()` from `mdsvex/compile`, given a `slug` or not, goes to the builder with `set_headings`. The editor reads the option from the manifest the plugin writes, or from `heading_ids: true` in an `mdsvex.config.json`, which can't hold a `slug` function.

### `smart_punctuation`

```ts
smart_punctuation:
	| boolean
	| { quotes?: boolean | [string, string, string, string]; dashes?: boolean; ellipses?: boolean }
	= false;
```

Turns the punctuation you can type into the punctuation a typesetter would use:

```js
mdsvex({ smart_punctuation: true });
```

| you write | you get | |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `"quoted"` | “quoted” | curly double quotes |
| `'quoted'`, `it's` | ‘quoted’, it’s | curly single quotes, and the apostrophe |
| `1990--2020` | 1990–2020 | two hyphens are an en dash |
| `wait --- what` | wait — what | three hyphens are an em dash |
| `and so on...` | and so on… | three dots are an ellipsis |

It is off by default, so a document renders the characters it has unless you ask. mdsvex 0.x had it on, see [smart punctuation in 0.x](docs#smart-punctuation-in-0x).

Only those five are replaced. A run of any other length stays as written, so four hyphens or four dots are still four. Two hyphens after a space and before a letter are a command line flag and stay too, so `run it with --watch` is safe, while `a -- b`, `a--b` and `1--2` get a dash.

A quote opens after a space, at the start of a paragraph, heading, list item or table cell, and after an opening bracket, a dash or another quote that just opened. Anywhere else it closes, which also makes an apostrophe. A quote with a space on both sides is left straight. Markup is looked through: the quotes in `_"word"_` and `"_word_"` face the same way, and the `'s` after a code span, a link or `{name}` is an apostrophe.

Quotes are not paired, so two cases come out wrong: an apostrophe at the start of a word opens (`'90s`, `rock 'n' roll`), and feet and inches close (`6'2"`). Type the character you want there, `’90s` or `6′2″`. A character you type yourself is never changed.

Each kind can be turned off, and `quotes` takes the four strings of another language, the opening and closing double quote and then the opening and closing single quote:

```js
mdsvex({
	smart_punctuation: {
		dashes: false,
		// French, with a no-break space inside the guillemets
		quotes: ['«\u00a0', '\u00a0»', '“', '”']
	}
});
```

German is `['„', '“', '‚', '‘']`. An apostrophe between two letters or digits is always `’`, whatever the closing single quote is. There is one style for the whole site, mdsvex does not read a document's language.

Only prose is touched. These stay exactly as written:

- code spans, fenced code, highlighted code and math
- Svelte expressions and blocks, such as `{"a--b"}` and `{#if x === "y"}`
- attributes of elements and components, such as `<Box --gap="1--2">`
- urls, and the text of an autolink such as `<https://example.com/a--b>`
- the `alt` text of an image and the `title` of a link or an image
- text inside `<code>`, `<kbd>`, `<pre>`, `<samp>`, `<var>`, `<tt>` and `<textarea>`, and the contents of `<script>` and `<style>`
- character references, so `&quot;` and `&#39;` are a way to keep a straight quote
- a character behind a backslash, so `\"`, `\'`, `\--` and `\...` stay literal
- frontmatter, HTML comments and thematic breaks

Text inside a component or any other element is prose and is changed.

[`heading_ids`](docs#heading_ids) makes its ids from the text as written, so turning this on does not change any anchor. `compile()` takes the option too. It belongs to the compile and to the incremental HTML renderer, so the streaming Svelte renderer shows text as written. The editor reads the option from the manifest the plugin writes, or from `smart_punctuation` in an `mdsvex.config.json`.

## Frontmatter

A document can start with YAML frontmatter between two `---` lines:

```svx
---
title: My lovely article
author: Dr. Fabuloso the Fabulous
tags: [svelte, markdown]
---

# {metadata.title}

By {metadata.author}.
```

The frontmatter is exported from the document's module script as `metadata`, so the document can read it and so can anything that imports the document:

```svelte
<script module>
	export const metadata = {
		title: 'My lovely article',
		author: 'Dr. Fabuloso the Fabulous',
		tags: ['svelte', 'markdown']
	};
</script>
```

```js
import { metadata } from './article.svx';
```

Keys are not separate variables, so write `{metadata.title}` rather than `{title}`. [Templates](docs#template-props) get every key as its own prop.

To list posts in SvelteKit, import their metadata with `import.meta.glob` and the `?metadata` query:

```js
// src/routes/blog/+page.js
const posts = import.meta.glob('./*/+page.svx', {
	query: '?metadata',
	import: 'metadata',
	eager: true
});

export function load() {
	return {
		posts: Object.entries(posts).map(([path, metadata]) => ({
			slug: path.split('/')[1],
			...metadata
		}))
	};
}
```

A `?metadata` import reads only the frontmatter, so the list does not load the component of every page.

A SvelteKit layout can read the frontmatter of the page it wraps the same way. Without `eager`, each page's metadata loads on its own, and the id of the route picks it:

```js
// src/routes/+layout.js
const pages = import.meta.glob('./**/+page.svx', {
	query: '?metadata',
	import: 'metadata'
});

export async function load({ route }) {
	// the root route is `/`, every other id has no trailing slash
	const dir = route.id === '/' ? '' : route.id;
	const metadata = await pages[`.${dir}/+page.svx`]?.();

	return { metadata: metadata ?? null };
}
```

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+layout.svelte -->
<script>
	let { data, children } = $props();
</script>

<svelte:head>
	<title>{data.metadata?.title ?? 'My site'}</title>
</svelte:head>

{@render children()}
```

`data.metadata` is `null` on a route that isn't a document, and on a document with no frontmatter. The same `load` works in a `+layout.server.js`.

### Supported YAML

mdsvex parses frontmatter itself, with no dependency, so `compile` still runs anywhere. It understands the YAML that frontmatter tends to use:

- plain, single quoted and double quoted strings, including escapes in double quotes;
- numbers (`1`, `-4.2`, `1e3`, `0x1F`, `0o17`, `.inf`, `.nan`), booleans and `null` or `~`, as in YAML 1.2, so `yes` and `off` stay strings;
- dates, which stay strings, such as `2026-10-02`;
- nested maps, block sequences (`- a`), flow sequences (`[a, b]`) and flow maps (`{ a: 1 }`);
- literal `|` and folded `>` blocks, with `-` and `+` chomping;
- comments.

These are errors that name the line, and point you at [`frontmatter.parse`](docs#custom-frontmatter-parsers):

- anchors (`&`), aliases (`*`), tags (`!!str`), complex keys (`?`) and merge keys (`<<`);
- indenting with tabs;
- a duplicate key;
- frontmatter that isn't a map of keys to values;
- a plain value containing `: `, such as `title: Hello: World`. Quote it: `title: 'Hello: World'`.

### Custom frontmatter parsers

`frontmatter.parse` replaces the built-in parser. It gets the raw text between the `---` lines and returns an object of metadata. Returning `null` or `undefined` means no metadata.

For full YAML, use a YAML library such as [`yaml`][yaml]:

```js
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { parse } from 'yaml';

mdsvex({
	frontmatter: { parse: (raw) => parse(raw) }
});
```

The fences stay `---`, but what goes between them is up to the parser. For TOML, with [`smol-toml`][smol-toml]:

```js
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { parse } from 'smol-toml';

mdsvex({
	frontmatter: { parse: (raw) => parse(raw) }
});
```

```svx
---
title = "TOML Example"

[owner]
name = "some name"
---
```

## Templates

A template is a Svelte component that wraps documents. It's the place for a document's title, byline and surrounding layout, and it can [replace the elements](docs#custom-components) of the documents it wraps. mdsvex 0.x called these layouts.

### Defining templates

Name your templates in the `templates` option:

```js
// vite.config.js
mdsvex({
	templates: {
		default: '#lib/templates/Post.svelte',
		docs: '#lib/templates/Docs.svelte'
	}
});
```

Each value is a specifier, resolved from the project root like an import, or a `URL`, such as `new URL('./src/Blog.svelte', import.meta.url)`.

A template renders the document with `{@render children()}`:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/templates/Post.svelte -->
<script>
	let { title, date, children } = $props();
</script>

<article>
	<h1>{title}</h1>
	<time datetime={date}>{date}</time>

	{@render children()}
</article>
```

Templates are ordinary components, so editing one hot reloads the documents that use it. See the [templates playground example](/playground/templates).

### Documents as templates

A template can be a document too, so its own content is markdown. Like a component, it reads its props and renders `children`. This is `src/lib/templates/Note.svx`:

```svx
<script>
	let { title, children } = $props();
</script>

# {title}

{@render children()}

Written by the _docs team_.
```

```js
mdsvex({
	templates: { note: '#lib/templates/Note.svx' }
});
```

It can [replace elements](docs#replacing-elements-in-a-template) from its `<script module>`, as a component does. As a template it is never wrapped in a template itself, so a `template` key in its frontmatter, `select_template` and the `default` entry don't apply to it.

### Choosing a template

mdsvex picks a document's template from the first of these that gives an answer:

1. The `?template=` query on the import, see [opting out](docs#opting-out).
2. The `template` key in the frontmatter, a template name or `false`.
3. `select_template(id, metadata)`, if you configured one.
4. The `default` entry in `templates`.
5. Otherwise, no template.

```svx
---
title: Writing docs
template: docs
---
```

An unknown name is an error that lists the templates you configured.

`select_template` runs for documents whose frontmatter has no `template` key. It gets the file path and the metadata. It returns a template name, `false` for none, or `undefined` to fall through to `default`:

```js
mdsvex({
	templates: {
		default: '#lib/templates/Post.svelte',
		docs: '#lib/templates/Docs.svelte'
	},
	select_template: (id) =>
		id.includes('/changelog/') ? false : id.includes('/docs/') ? 'docs' : undefined
});
```

### Opting out

`template: false` in the frontmatter renders a document without one:

```svx
---
template: false
---
```

To choose for one import only, add `?template=` to it. `?template=false` unwraps the document and `?template=docs` picks another template. The query wins over the frontmatter:

```svelte
<script>
	import Intro from './intro.svx?template=false';
</script>

<Intro />
```

### Template props

A template gets:

- every frontmatter key as its own prop, so `let { title } = $props()` reads `title`;
- `plugin_data`, what the plugins left for the document, when they left any (see [plugin data](docs#plugin-data));
- `children`, a snippet that renders the document;
- the props passed to the document, so `<Post featured />` reaches the template as `featured`.

The frontmatter can't have a `children` key when the document has a template, nor a `plugin_data` key when its plugins left data. Each is an error that points at the line.

A document that reads its own props with `let props = $props()` still forwards them all to its template. If it destructures them, `let { featured } = $props()`, it forwards none, as `$props()` can only be called once.

### What gets hoisted

Some things only work at the top level of a component, so they stay outside the template:

- `<script>` and `<script module>`;
- `<style>`;
- `<svelte:head>`, `<svelte:window>`, `<svelte:body>`, `<svelte:document>` and `<svelte:options>`.

Everything else, markdown, HTML and components alike, is the document body that the template renders as `children`.

## Custom components

Custom components replace the elements markdown produces. A `## heading` can render your `Heading.svelte`, and a fenced code block your `CodeBlock.svelte`.

### Replacing elements in a template

A template's `<script module>` exports replace elements by name, in the documents it wraps:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/templates/Docs.svelte -->
<script module>
	export { default as h2 } from './docs/Heading.svelte';
	export { default as pre } from './docs/CodeBlock.svelte';
</script>

<script>
	let { title, children } = $props();
</script>

<main class="docs">
	<h1>{title}</h1>
	{@render children()}
</main>
```

The export name is the element name. mdsvex reads the names without running the module, so export each one by name. A name `export *` re-exports can't be seen, and mdsvex warns about it.

For a template you can't edit, such as one from a package, give the replacements in a module of their own. They are merged over the template's own:

```js
mdsvex({
	templates: {
		theme: {
			component: '@acme/theme/Layout.svelte',
			components: '#lib/theme-overrides.js'
		}
	}
});
```

### The `components` option

`components` names one or more modules whose exports replace elements in every document, including documents with no template:

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as img } from './Image.svelte';
export { default as a } from './Link.svelte';
```

```js
mdsvex({ components: '#lib/markdown.js' });
```

See the [templates playground example](/playground/templates), which uses both.

### Precedence

For each element, mdsvex uses the first match from:

1. the document's template, including its `components` override module;
2. the `components` modules, the last module in the list winning;
3. no match, the plain element.

`template: false` documents only use the `components` modules.

Markdown inside a component you use, such as `<Aside>`, uses the same replacements as the rest of the document.

### Element props

Every replacement gets the element's attributes as props, including any a parse plugin added, such as an `id`. Unless the element is void, it gets its content as `children`. Some elements get more:

| element | extra props |
| --- | --- |
| `h1` to `h6` | `level`, a number from 1 to 6 |
| `a` | `href`, `title` |
| `img` | `src`, `alt`, `title`, and no `children` |
| `pre` | `lang`, `meta`, `code`, `title`, `caption` and meta props, see [code block components](docs#code-block-components) |
| `code` | inline code only, its text as `children`, and `lang` when it is [highlighted](docs#inline-code) |
| `ol` | `start` |
| `li` | `checked`, `true` or `false` on a task list item (`- [x] done`), and no checkbox in its `children` |
| `th`, `td` | `align`, `"left"`, `"right"` or `"center"` |

`hr` and `br` are void too. For a fenced code block, `children` is the whole highlighted `<pre>` element.

One component can serve several elements:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/templates/docs/Heading.svelte -->
<script>
	let { level, id, children } = $props();
</script>

<svelte:element this={`h${level}`} {id}>
	<a href="#{id}">#</a>
	{@render children()}
</svelte:element>
```

```js
export { default as h2, default as h3 } from './docs/Heading.svelte';
```

Svelte ignores props a component doesn't declare, so a replacement only reads what it needs.

### `component_mode: 'all'`

By default, only elements written as markdown are replaced, so typing HTML such as `<a>` gets you the plain element. With `component_mode: 'all'`, lowercase HTML you type is replaced as well:

```js
mdsvex({
	components: '#lib/markdown.js',
	component_mode: 'all'
});
```

```svx
<a href="/about">Replaced by the a export</a>

<svelte:element this={'a'} href="/about">Never replaced</svelte:element>
```

- `<svelte:element this={'a'}>` is the escape hatch, it is never replaced.
- `<svelte:*>` elements and capitalised components are never replaced.
- An element with a directive, such as `bind:`, `on:`, `use:`, `class:`, `style:`, `transition:`, `in:`, `out:`, `animate:` or `let:`, stays a plain element even when it has a replacement, because a component can't take a directive. mdsvex warns, naming the element and the directive. Event attributes such as `onclick` are plain props and are passed along.

### Lowercase custom tags

With `component_mode: 'all'`, a lowercase tag matching an export is replaced too. A `warning` export gives every document a `<warning>` tag, with no import:

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as warning } from './Warning.svelte';
```

```svx
<warning type="tip">

Markdown *inside* still works.

</warning>
```

A tag name that isn't a JavaScript identifier, such as `<info-box>`, matches a string export: `export { default as 'info-box' } from './InfoBox.svelte'`. Capitalised tags are always plain Svelte components, imported as usual. See the [all mode playground example](/playground/all-mode).

### Nested scopes

Markdown inside a component always uses the document's replacements, never ones the wrapping component supplies. Letting `<Aside>` change how the markdown inside it renders is deferred for now.

Until then, move that markdown into a document of its own, and give it a template with the replacements you want. Here that is an `aside` entry in `templates`, which `?template=` picks on the import:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/templates/Aside.svelte -->
<script module>
	export { default as p } from './aside/Paragraph.svelte';
</script>

<script>
	let { children } = $props();
</script>

{@render children()}
```

```svx
<script>
	import AsideText from './aside-text.svx?template=aside';
</script>

## Header 2

<aside>
	<AsideText />
</aside>
```

## Directives

Directives give markdown a syntax for your own components, without writing HTML. Each one renders a Svelte component:

```svx
:::Callout[Heads up](kind=warn)
Body *markdown*
:::
```

See the [directives playground example](/playground/directives).

### Declaring directives

Directives come from a `directives` namespace export. Any module that can replace elements can have one, the `components` modules and a template's `<script module>` alike:

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as img } from './Image.svelte';
export * as directives from './directives.js';
```

```js
// src/lib/directives.js
export { default as Callout } from './Callout.svelte';
export { default as youtube } from './YouTube.svelte';
```

A directive's name matches an export name exactly, so `:::Callout` uses `Callout`. Directives and elements are separate, so a `table` directive never replaces tables, and an element export never serves a directive.

Write it as `export * as directives from '...'`, so mdsvex can read the names without running the module. Any other `directives` export is an error.

A template's directives come before those of the `components` modules, in the same order as [element replacements](docs#precedence).

### The three forms

| form | example | props |
| --- | --- | --- |
| inline | `:abbr[HTML](title=HyperText)` | args, and the text in brackets as `children` |
| leaf | `::youtube[A talk](id=dQw4w9WgXcQ)`, `::toc` | args, and the text in brackets as `label` |
| container | `:::Callout[Heads up](kind=warn)` ... `:::`, `:::note` ... `:::` | args, the text in brackets as `label` and the body as `children` |

A leaf or container takes a line of its own, and a container ends at a line of colons. Its brackets are optional: `::toc` is the same as `::toc[]`, and args can follow the name directly, as in `::toc(depth=2)`. Nothing else can follow on the line, so `::toc is here` is a paragraph. A line that is only `::word` or `:::word` is a directive and needs a component, write `\::word` to keep it as text.

An inline directive always needs its brackets, which can be empty: `:icon[]`. Without them a `:tada:` or `std::vector` in a sentence would be a directive.

Containers nest when the outer one uses more colons. The bracket text can hold emphasis, strong, code and other directives, but links and images in it stay plain text.

A name starts with a letter, then letters, digits, `_` or `-`. A name that isn't a JavaScript identifier, such as `info-box`, matches a string export: `export { default as 'info-box' } from './InfoBox.svelte'`.

### Directive props

- Args in parentheses become string props: `(kind=warn, title="A title")` gives `kind="warn"` and `title="A title"`. Values are never Svelte expressions.
- `label` is a snippet of the bracket text of a leaf or container. Its inline markdown is rendered, and empty or missing brackets pass no `label`.
- `children` is a snippet of an inline directive's bracket text, or of a container's body.

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/Callout.svelte -->
<script>
	let { kind = 'note', label, children } = $props();
</script>

<aside class="callout {kind}">
	{#if label}<strong>{@render label()}</strong>{/if}
	{@render children?.()}
</aside>
```

An arg named `children` is an error. So is `label` on a leaf or container, though an inline directive can take a `label` arg as a plain prop.

### Unknown directives

A directive with no component is an error that names it and its line and column:

```
no component renders the directive :::Calout at 3:1. Export a component named "Calout" from a directives module (export * as directives from './directives.ts'), or replace the directive_container node in a parse plugin
```

A [parse plugin](docs#parse_plugins) can handle directives that have no component, through the `directive_inline`, `directive_leaf` and `directive_container` node types. A directive with a component never reaches those handlers.

### Directives from a plugin

A [plugin](docs#plugins) can bring directives of its own, with the components that render them. They sit below everything above: an export of the same name from a `components` module or a template replaces the plugin's component. [`@mdsvex/plugin-steps`](docs#steps) is one.

## Plugins

The first-party plugins are packages of their own, named `@mdsvex/plugin-*`. Each exports a function, and what it returns goes in the [`plugins`](docs#plugins) option:

```js
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { anchors } from '@mdsvex/plugin-anchors';
import { citations } from '@mdsvex/plugin-citations';
import { emoji } from '@mdsvex/plugin-emoji';
import { external_links } from '@mdsvex/plugin-external-links';
import { page_data } from '@mdsvex/plugin-page-data';
import { references } from '@mdsvex/plugin-references';
import { steps } from '@mdsvex/plugin-steps';
import { tabs } from '@mdsvex/plugin-tabs';
import { toc } from '@mdsvex/plugin-toc';

mdsvex({
	heading_ids: true,
	plugins: [
		steps(),
		tabs(),
		anchors(),
		external_links(),
		toc(),
		page_data(),
		references({ repo: 'you/site' }),
		emoji(),
		citations()
	]
});
```

| package | what it adds |
| --- | --- |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-steps`](docs#steps) | numbered steps, one for each heading inside `:::steps` |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-tabs`](docs#tabs) | tabs and code groups, one tab for each heading or code fence inside `:::tabs` |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-anchors`](docs#anchors) | a link to itself on every heading that has an id |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-external-links`](docs#external-links) | `rel` and `target` on every link that leaves the site |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-toc`](docs#table-of-contents) | the headings of a document as data, and a `::toc` directive that lists them |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-page-data`](docs#page-data) | a reading time, an excerpt, an inferred title, an edit url and a last modified date for each document |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-emoji`](docs#emoji) | emoji shortcodes, `:tada:` becomes the emoji, with an optional label for screen readers |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-references`](docs#references) | links for `@user`, `#123`, `owner/repo#123` and commits, and for bare urls when asked |
| [`@mdsvex/plugin-citations`](docs#citations) | `:cite[key]` from a CSL JSON bibliography, and a `::bibliography` that lists what a document cites |

### Steps

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-steps@next
```

```js
import { steps } from '@mdsvex/plugin-steps';

mdsvex({ plugins: [steps()] });
```

Each heading written directly inside `:::steps` starts a step. The heading is its title, and what follows, up to the next such heading, is its body:

````svx
:::steps

## Install

```sh
pnpm add mdsvex
```

## Configure

Add it to the config.

:::
````

```html
<ol class="steps" role="list">
	<li class="step">
		<div class="step-title"><h2>Install</h2></div>
		<div class="step-body"><pre>...</pre></div>
	</li>
	<li class="step">
		<div class="step-title"><h2>Configure</h2></div>
		<div class="step-body"><p>Add it to the config.</p></div>
	</li>
</ol>
```

- **The first heading sets the depth.** If it is a `##`, every `##` written directly inside the container starts a step. A heading of any other depth is content of the step it is in.
- **`(level=3)` names the depth instead**, as in `:::steps(level=3)`, whatever the first heading is.
- **Only headings written directly inside count.** One in a block quote, a list or another directive starts no step.
- **Content before the first heading is in no step.** It stays at the top of the list, where only list items belong, so put an introduction above the container or in its brackets: `:::steps[Set up a project]` renders that text in a `<p class="steps-label">` ahead of the list.
- **Steps nest** when the outer container uses more colons, and the inner list counts from 1.
- **A step can be written out** as `:::step[Its title]` inside the container, beside the ones headings make.

The titles are still headings, so [`heading_ids`](docs#heading_ids) and [anchors](docs#anchors) reach them.

The numbers are drawn by a small stylesheet that comes with the components, from a CSS counter. Its selectors have no specificity, so any rule of yours wins, and it reads these custom properties from `.steps`:

| property | default |
| --- | --- |
| `--steps-marker-size` | `1.75rem` |
| `--steps-gap` | `0.75rem` |
| `--steps-marker-color` | `inherit` |
| `--steps-marker-background` | the text colour at 12% |
| `--steps-line` | the text colour at 20% |

`steps({ css: false })` leaves the stylesheet out and keeps the markup, which is then a plain numbered list. Write your own from there, or start from a copy of `@mdsvex/plugin-steps/steps.css`.

To change the markup, export your own `steps` or `step` from a [directives module](docs#declaring-directives). It replaces the plugin's component and gets the same props, `label` (the title, or the bracket text of the container) and `children`:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/Step.svelte -->
<script>
	let { label, children } = $props();
</script>

<li class="step">
	<header>{@render label?.()}</header>
	{@render children?.()}
</li>
```

```js
// src/lib/directives.js
export { default as step } from './Step.svelte';
```

The plugin's own components can be imported from `@mdsvex/plugin-steps/Steps.svelte` and `@mdsvex/plugin-steps/Step.svelte`, to wrap one in yours.

### Tabs

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-tabs@next
```

```js
import { tabs } from '@mdsvex/plugin-tabs';

mdsvex({ plugins: [tabs()] });
```

Each heading written directly inside `:::tabs` starts a tab. The text of the heading is its label, and what follows, up to the next such heading, is its panel:

````svx
:::tabs

## macOS

Install it with Homebrew.

## Windows

Run the installer.

:::
````

```html
<div class="tabs">
	<div class="tabs-list" role="tablist">
		<button class="tab" role="tab" aria-selected="true">macOS</button>
		<button class="tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false">Windows</button>
	</div>
	<div class="tab-panel" role="tabpanel">
		<div class="tab-panel-label" hidden>macOS</div>
		<p>Install it with Homebrew.</p>
	</div>
	<div class="tab-panel" role="tabpanel" hidden>
		<div class="tab-panel-label" hidden>Windows</div>
		<p>Run the installer.</p>
	</div>
</div>
```

- **The first heading sets the depth**, as it does for [steps](docs#steps). A heading of any other depth is content of the panel it is in, and one in a block quote, a list or another directive starts no tab.
- **A label is not a heading in the page.** It is rendered inside the tab button as inline content, with its emphasis, code and expressions kept. It gets no id from [`heading_ids`](docs#heading_ids) and no [anchor](docs#anchors), since a panel that may be hidden is not a section a reader can jump to.
- **`:::tabs[Pick your system]`** renders that text in a `<p class="tabs-label">` above the tabs and names the tab list with it.
- **Tabs nest** when the outer container uses more colons.

**A code group** is the same directive around code fences. The first heading or fence written directly inside the container decides which it is. When that is a fence, each fence written directly inside is a tab, labelled by its name (`[npm]` or `title="npm"`, as [fence meta](docs#fence-meta) reads them) or, with neither, by its language:

````svx
:::tabs

```sh [npm]
npm install mdsvex
```

```sh [pnpm]
pnpm add mdsvex
```

:::
````

The fence is rendered as it is anywhere else, by the highlighter or by a [replaced `pre`](docs#code-block-components), which still gets `title`. The stylesheet hides the `<figcaption>` the highlighter makes for a title, since the tab shows it. Anything after a fence is in its panel, up to the next fence.

**`(group=name)`** makes tab sets switch together. Choosing a tab in one set chooses the tab with the same label in every set of that group, and the choice is kept in `localStorage` for the next page:

```svx
:::tabs(group=pm)

## npm

## pnpm

:::
```

The server never knows the choice. It renders the first tab open, the first render in the browser does too, and the stored choice is applied after hydration, so the two never disagree.

The components follow the [WAI-ARIA tabs pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/tabs/): the roles, `aria-selected` and `aria-controls`, one tab in the tab order, the arrow keys to move between tabs with <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd>, and a panel that takes focus.

**Without JavaScript** the stylesheet hides the tab list and shows every panel, one after another, each under its label, since buttons that can't be pressed would hide all but the first. It uses the `scripting` media query, so an older browser shows the first panel only. Before a page hydrates a reader sees the first panel, laid out as it will stay.

The build warns, with the line, about a `:::tabs` with no tabs (`no_tabs`), a tab with no label (`tab_without_label`, the tab is labelled by its number) and content before the first tab (`content_before_first_tab`, it stays above the panels).

The stylesheet that comes with the components has selectors with no specificity, so any rule of yours wins, and reads these custom properties from `.tabs`:

| property | default |
| --- | --- |
| `--tabs-gap` | `0.25rem` |
| `--tabs-padding` | `0.5rem 0.75rem` |
| `--tabs-line` | the text colour at 20% |
| `--tabs-color` | the text colour at 70% |
| `--tabs-selected-color` | `inherit` |
| `--tabs-selected-line` | the text colour |

`tabs({ css: false })` leaves it out and keeps the markup. To change the markup, export your own `tabs` and `tab` from a [directives module](docs#declaring-directives). They get `label` and `children`, a `tabs` also gets `group` and `code` (`"true"` for a code group), and a `tab` gets `index`. The `label` of a tab is the content of its heading, or the name of its fence as text. The plugin's own are at `@mdsvex/plugin-tabs/Tabs.svelte` and `@mdsvex/plugin-tabs/Tab.svelte`. It needs Svelte 5.20 or later.

### Anchors

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-anchors@next
```

```js
import { anchors } from '@mdsvex/plugin-anchors';

mdsvex({ heading_ids: true, plugins: [anchors()] });
```

Puts a link to itself on every heading that has an id:

```html
<h2 id="getting-started"><a href="#getting-started">Getting started</a></h2>
```

**It needs [`heading_ids`](docs#heading_ids).** The plugin makes no ids of its own. It links the id each heading has once `heading_ids` has run, so with the option off no heading has one and nothing is linked. A heading left without an id, such as `## {title}`, gets no link either. An id a [parse plugin](docs#parse_plugins) set is linked like any other.

```ts
anchors({
	position: 'wrap' | 'prepend' | 'append' = 'wrap',
	attrs: Record<string, string>
});
```

- **`'wrap'`** puts the content of the heading inside the link. A heading that already holds a link is left as it is, since a link can't hold another.
- **`'prepend'`** and **`'append'`** add an empty link before or after the content, with `aria-hidden="true"` and `tabindex="-1"`, for a stylesheet to draw:

```js
anchors({ position: 'prepend', attrs: { class: 'anchor' } });
```

```html
<h2 id="getting-started">
	<a href="#getting-started" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" class="anchor"></a>Getting started
</h2>
```

```css
.anchor::before {
	content: '#';
}
```

- **`attrs`** are more attributes for the link. One named as a default replaces it.

### External links

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-external-links@next
```

```js
import { external_links } from '@mdsvex/plugin-external-links';

mdsvex({ plugins: [external_links()] });
```

Sets `rel`, and `target` when you ask for one, on every markdown link that leaves the site:

```svx
Read [the spec](https://example.com/spec), then [the guide](/guide).
```

```html
<p>
	Read <a href="https://example.com/spec" rel="noopener noreferrer">the spec</a>,
	then <a href="/guide">the guide</a>.
</p>
```

A link is external when its url is absolute, with an `http:` or `https:` scheme, or protocol relative (`//cdn.example.com/x`), and its host is not one of `internal_hosts`. Autolinks count. Everything else is left as it was written: relative urls, `#fragments`, `mailto:` and other schemes, and a url whose host comes from an expression, such as `{url}` or `{base}/docs`, since the plugin can't know at build time where it goes. An expression after the host is fine: `https://example.com/{slug}` is external.

**It sees markdown links only.** An `<a href>` you typed as HTML is an element, not a link, and is rendered as you wrote it. A link a component renders is not seen either.

```ts
external_links({
	target: string,
	rel: string | string[] = ['noopener', 'noreferrer'],
	internal_hosts: string[],
	is_external: (href: string, external: boolean) => boolean,
	attrs: Record<string, string>,
	icon: boolean | Record<string, string>,
	new_tab_label: string
});
```

- **`target`** is not set by default, so an external link opens where the reader chooses. `target: '_blank'` opens a new tab.
- **`rel`** tokens are added to the ones a link already has. `noreferrer` keeps the address of your page out of the request to the other site. `noopener` is implied by it and by `target="_blank"` in current browsers, and is written out for older ones. `nofollow` is left out because it suits links your users wrote, not your own. `rel: []` adds none.
- **`internal_hosts`** are the hosts of your own site, such as `['example.com', 'www.example.com']`. A host is compared whole, without its port.
- **`is_external`** decides for each link in place of the built in test, whose answer is its second argument: `(href, external) => external || href.endsWith('.pdf')`.
- **`attrs`** are more attributes for an external link, such as a class.
- **`icon`** appends an empty `<span class="external-link-icon" aria-hidden="true">` to each external link for a stylesheet to draw. An object is the attributes of the span.
- **`new_tab_label`** is what a screen reader says after a link that opens a new tab. It needs a `target` that opens one:

```js
external_links({
	target: '_blank',
	icon: true,
	new_tab_label: 'opens in a new tab'
});
```

```html
<a href="https://example.com/spec" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
	the spec<span class="external-link-icon" role="img" aria-label="opens in a new tab"></span>
</a>
```

The label is the name of the span, read out as part of the link the way the `alt` of an icon would be. There is no default, since the plugin can't know the language of the page.

The plugin reads each link as it closes, so `rel` and `target` are there while a document is still streaming. It keeps a `target` the link already has and adds to its `rel`, so a plugin that sets either has to come before it in `plugins`. A [replaced `a`](docs#custom-components) gets `rel` and `target` as props.

### Table of contents

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-toc@next
```

```js
import { toc } from '@mdsvex/plugin-toc';

mdsvex({ heading_ids: true, plugins: [toc()] });
```

Leaves the headings of each document in its [plugin data](docs#plugin-data), as a flat list in the order they are written:

```js
import Page, { plugin_data } from './getting-started.svx';

plugin_data.toc.headings;
// [
//   { depth: 2, id: 'install', text: 'Install', position: { start, end } },
//   { depth: 3, id: 'with-pnpm', text: 'With pnpm', position: { start, end } },
//   { depth: 2, id: 'configure', text: 'Configure', position: { start, end } }
// ]
```

`text` is what a reader sees, without markup, and `position` is where the heading is in the source. A document with nothing to list has no `toc` key. `toc_tree(headings)`, from the same package, nests the list, each heading under the nearest less deep one before it.

**It needs [`heading_ids`](docs#heading_ids).** The plugin makes no ids of its own. With the option off, the compile of a document that has a heading to list fails with a `TocError`. A heading left without an id, such as `## {title}`, is left out with a warning.

```ts
toc({
	min_depth: number = 2,
	max_depth: number = 3
});
```

The defaults leave out the `#` title of the page and anything below `###`. To leave one heading out, put a `<!-- no-toc -->` comment in it. It keeps its id:

```svx
## Changelog <!-- no-toc -->
```

Every other markdown heading inside the depths is listed, wherever it is written. The headings of [steps](docs#steps) are listed. The headings that start a [tab](docs#tabs) are labels, not headings, and are not. The build warns about a heading that skips a level, such as a `###` straight after a `#`.

The package ships a component that renders the list, nested. A [template](docs#template-props) gives it the data:

```svelte
<script>
	import Toc from '@mdsvex/plugin-toc/Toc.svelte';

	let { plugin_data, children } = $props();
</script>

<aside>
	<Toc headings={plugin_data?.toc?.headings} aria-label="On this page" />
</aside>
<main>{@render children()}</main>
```

```html
<nav aria-label="On this page" class="toc">
	<ul>
		<li>
			<a href="#install">Install</a>
			<ul>
				<li><a href="#with-pnpm">With pnpm</a></li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li><a href="#configure">Configure</a></li>
	</ul>
</nav>
```

It takes `min_depth` and `max_depth` to narrow the list, a `label` snippet, and passes anything else to the `<nav>`. It needs Svelte 5.20 or later and ships no styles.

`::toc`, on a line of its own, renders the same component in the document:

```svx
# Getting started

::toc[On this page]

## Install
```

The brackets are its label, `::toc(max_depth=2)` narrows it, and any other arg is an attribute of the `<nav>`. It lists the whole document, the headings after it as well: the plugin fills it in once the document is parsed and its ids are set. While a document streams, nothing is shown in its place until the stream ends. A `::toc` with nothing to list renders nothing, and the build says so.

### Page data

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-page-data@next
```

```js
import { page_data } from '@mdsvex/plugin-page-data';

mdsvex({ plugins: [page_data()] });
```

Works out facts about each document and leaves them in its [plugin data](docs#plugin-data), under one key:

```js
import Post, { plugin_data } from './moving-to-runes.svx';

plugin_data.page;
// {
//   title: 'Moving to runes',
//   excerpt: 'Signals changed how I write components. Here is what I learned.',
//   words: 842,
//   minutes: 5,
//   edit_url: 'https://github.com/you/site/edit/main/src/routes/blog/moving-to-runes.svx',
//   last_modified: '2026-10-15T09:30:00.000Z'
// }
```

`metadata` is not touched, it stays exactly the frontmatter. Each part can be turned off. `title`, `excerpt`, `edit_url` and `last_modified` are only there when their part found something, and a document with no prose has `words: 0` and `minutes: 0`:

```ts
page_data({
	reading_time: boolean | { words_per_minute: number } = true,
	excerpt: boolean | { max_length: number, ellipsis: string } = true,
	title: boolean = true,
	edit_url: string | { base: string, root: string } | ((filename: string) => string | undefined),
	last_modified: 'git' | 'fs' | ((filename: string) => Promise<string | Date | undefined>)
});
```

- **`words`** counts the prose: headings, paragraphs, lists, quotes and table cells. Code blocks, image alts, comments, expressions and attributes are not counted, and code inside a sentence is. Chinese, Japanese and Korean are written without spaces, so each Han ideograph, hiragana and hangul syllable is a word, the rule of the `reading-time` package.
- **`minutes`** is `words` over `words_per_minute`, 200 by default, to two decimals and then rounded up, as `reading-time` shows it. One word is 1 minute and 202 words are 2.
- **`excerpt`** is the first paragraph, or everything before a `<!-- more -->` comment when the document has one. It is plain text: a parse plugin sees the document before it is rendered, so there is no HTML to give. `max_length` cuts a longer one where a word ends and adds the `ellipsis`.
- **`title`** is the text of the first `#` heading, and is only set when the frontmatter has no `title`.
- **`edit_url`** is off until you give it a base. The url is the base and the path of the document relative to `root`, the working directory by default:

```js
page_data({ edit_url: 'https://github.com/you/site/edit/main/' });
```

- **`last_modified`** is off until you choose where the date comes from. `'git'` is the date of the last commit that touched the document, `'fs'` is the modification time of its file, and a function is given the path of the document and returns the date. It is an ISO string in UTC:

```js
page_data({ last_modified: 'git' });
```

`'git'` and `'fs'` need Node, and throw a `PageDataError` when the plugin is made anywhere else. `'git'` reads the history of the repository once with one `git log`, for every document, so a site of a thousand pages runs git twice, not a thousand times. A file that is not committed yet has no date. A shallow clone, which CI makes by default, cuts the history, so a file whose last commit is before the cut has no date and is reported as `shallow_history`: fetch the whole history (`fetch-depth: 0` in GitHub Actions). In the dev server the history is read once, so a commit made while it runs shows after a restart. `'fs'` gives the time of the checkout in CI, so it is for a site built where it is written.

Finding the date waits, so the Vite plugin and `compile_async` take a document with `last_modified` on, and `compile` throws a `PluginAsyncError` for it.

A [`?data` import](docs#plugin-data) gives all of it without rendering the document, which is what a page that lists posts wants:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/blog/+page.svelte -->
<script>
	const posts = import.meta.glob('./*/+page.svx', { query: '?data', eager: true });
</script>

<ul>
	{#each Object.entries(posts) as [path, { metadata, plugin_data }] (path)}
		<li>
			<a href="/blog/{path.split('/')[1]}">{metadata?.title ?? plugin_data?.page?.title}</a>
			<span>{plugin_data?.page?.minutes} min read</span>
			<p>{plugin_data?.page?.excerpt}</p>
		</li>
	{/each}
</ul>
```

### Emoji

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-emoji@next
```

```js
import { emoji } from '@mdsvex/plugin-emoji';

mdsvex({ plugins: [emoji()] });
```

Turns a shortcode into its emoji:

```svx
We shipped it :tada: and nobody was paged :+1:
```

```html
<p>We shipped it 🎉 and nobody was paged 👍</p>
```

The shortcodes are the ones GitHub uses, 1913 names for 1870 emoji. The table is part of the package, built from [`gemoji`](https://github.com/wooorm/gemoji) (MIT), so there is nothing else to install.

A shortcode is replaced in prose and nowhere else: not in code, math, a url, the value of an attribute or an `{expression}`. What is not a shortcode stays as you wrote it:

- a name the table does not have, such as `:not_an_emoji:`
- a name that touches a letter or a digit, so `10:30:00` and `1:100:1` are safe although `:100:` is an emoji
- a shortcode you escaped, `\:tada:`
- an inline directive, `:tada[text]`, whose name is followed by a bracket and not a colon

```ts
emoji({
	table: Record<string, string>,
	extend: Record<string, string | null>,
	label: boolean | ((emoji: string, name: string | undefined) => string | undefined),
	attrs: Record<string, string>
});
```

- **`extend`** adds shortcodes to the table, replaces one of the same name, and takes one out with `null`: `{ shipit: '...', 100: null }`.
- **`table`** is the whole table in place of the built in one.
- **`label`** wraps each emoji in a `<span>` with a name for a screen reader. `true` uses the Unicode name of the emoji, and a function gives the name, for a page that is not in English. Emoji you typed as characters are wrapped as well:

```js
emoji({ label: true });
```

```html
<p>We shipped it <span role="img" aria-label="party popper">🎉</span></p>
```

- **`attrs`** are attributes for a `<span>` around each emoji, such as a class, with or without a label.

It writes the character and adds no image set, and emoticons such as `:)` are left alone. A [heading id](docs#heading_ids) is made from what you wrote, so `## Done :tada:` gets `done-tada`. The plugin reads the finished tree, so put it after a plugin that turns urls into links, such as [references](docs#references) with `linkify`.

### References

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-references@next
```

```js
import { references } from '@mdsvex/plugin-references';

mdsvex({ plugins: [references({ repo: 'sveltejs/svelte' })] });
```

Turns the references people write in prose into links:

```svx
Thanks @ada for #123, which also closes sveltejs/kit#45. Landed in a1b2c3d.
```

```html
<p>
	Thanks <a href="https://github.com/ada">@ada</a> for
	<a href="https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/issues/123">#123</a>, which also
	closes <a href="https://github.com/sveltejs/kit/issues/45">sveltejs/kit#45</a>.
	Landed in
	<a href="https://github.com/sveltejs/svelte/commit/a1b2c3d"><code>a1b2c3d</code></a>.
</p>
```

| written | becomes a link to | needs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `@user` | the user | |
| `owner/repo#123` | that issue or pull request | |
| `#123` | the issue or pull request in your repo | `repo` |
| a commit hash of 7 to 40 characters | the commit in your repo | `repo` |
| `https://example.com`, `www.example.com` | itself | `linkify: true` |

A reference has to stand on its own, so an email address, a path such as `/@ada`, `page#12` and a word like `defaced` are left alone, and so is one you escaped, `\@ada`. Nothing is touched in code, math, an attribute, an `{expression}`, or the text of a link you already wrote.

```ts
references({
	repo: string,
	mention: string | false | ((parts: { user }) => string | undefined),
	issue: string | false | ((parts: { repo, owner, name, number }) => string | undefined),
	commit: string | false | ((parts: { repo, owner, name, sha }) => string | undefined),
	linkify: boolean,
	attrs: Record<string, string> | ((kind) => Record<string, string> | undefined)
});
```

- **`repo`** is the repository a bare `#123` and a commit belong to, as `owner/name`. Without it neither is linked.
- **`mention`**, **`issue`** and **`commit`** are the url of each kind. The defaults are GitHub's, such as `https://github.com/{repo}/issues/{number}`. A string takes placeholders, a function returns the url or nothing to leave the reference as text, and `false` turns the kind off. For another host:

```js
references({
	repo: 'group/project',
	mention: 'https://gitlab.com/{user}',
	issue: 'https://gitlab.com/{repo}/-/issues/{number}',
	commit: 'https://gitlab.com/{repo}/-/commit/{sha}'
});
```

- **`linkify`** also makes a link of a bare `https://`, `http://` or `www.` url. The parser never does, on purpose: in mdsvex a url is a link when you mark it, `<https://example.com>`, and this option is where you opt in to the rest. Punctuation at the end of a url stays with the sentence, by the rules GitHub uses.
- **`attrs`** are attributes for each link, or a function of the kind (`'mention'`, `'issue'`, `'commit'`, `'url'`) that returns them.

It does not check that a user or an issue exists. With `linkify`, list it before the emoji plugin, so a shortcode inside a url is left alone. [External links](docs#external-links) reads links while the document is parsed and does not see the ones made here, so give them `rel` through `attrs`.

`compile()` takes every plugin the same way, through its `plugins` option.

### Citations

```bash
pnpm add --save-dev @mdsvex/plugin-citations@next
```

```js
import { citations } from '@mdsvex/plugin-citations';

mdsvex({ plugins: [citations()] });
```

Cites entries of a [CSL JSON](https://citeproc-js.readthedocs.io/en/latest/csl-json/markup.html) file, which the frontmatter names, and lists the entries the document cites where you write `::bibliography`:

```svx
---
bibliography: ./refs.json
---

The engine could do more than count :cite[lovelace1843], as was later proved :cite[turing1936, lovelace1843].

::bibliography[References]
```

```html
<p>
	The engine could do more than count
	<span class="citation">[<a href="#bib-lovelace1843" id="cite-1" role="doc-biblioref">1</a>]</span>,
	as was later proved
	<span class="citation">[<a href="#bib-turing1936" id="cite-2" role="doc-biblioref">2</a>,
	<a href="#bib-lovelace1843" id="cite-3" role="doc-biblioref">1</a>]</span>.
</p>
<section role="doc-bibliography" aria-labelledby="s1-label" class="bibliography">
	<p class="bibliography-label" id="s1-label">References</p>
	<ol>
		<li id="bib-lovelace1843">
			Ada Lovelace. <cite>Notes on the Analytical Engine.</cite> Scientific Memoirs 3, 666-731. 1843.
			<a href="#cite-1" role="doc-backlink" aria-label="Back to citation 1">↩︎</a>
			<a href="#cite-3" role="doc-backlink" aria-label="Back to citation 3">↩︎</a>
		</li>
		<li id="bib-turing1936">...</li>
	</ol>
</section>
```

```ts
citations({
	bibliography: string, // the file of a document whose frontmatter names none
	style: 'numeric' | 'author-date' = 'numeric'
});
```

- **`:cite[key]`** cites one entry and `:cite[a, b]` several, by the `id` of each in the file. Each key links to its entry and each entry links back to every citation of it.
- **`style`** is `numeric`, `[1]` and a list numbered by first citation, or `author-date`, `(Lovelace 1843; Turing 1936)` and a list by author.
- **The file** is the `bibliography` key of the frontmatter, relative to the document, or the `bibliography` option, absolute or relative to the working directory. It is read once for every document and read again when it changes, in the dev server too. A document that cites with no file named fails with a `CitationsError`.
- **An unknown key** is a warning at the citation, which shows the key as written, without a link.
- **BibTeX** is not read. Zotero, JabRef and `pandoc refs.bib -t csljson -o refs.json` export CSL JSON.

The citations and their entries are [plugin data](docs#plugin-data), `plugin_data.citations`, so a template can render the list itself with the `Bibliography` component:

```svelte
<script>
	import Bibliography from '@mdsvex/plugin-citations/Bibliography.svelte';
	let { plugin_data, children } = $props();
</script>

{@render children()}
<Bibliography data={plugin_data?.citations} />
```

The file is read while the document compiles, so the Vite plugin and [`compile_async`](docs#compile_async), with a `read` option, take a document that cites, and `compile` throws a `PluginAsyncError`. The formatting is small on purpose: names, year, title, where it was published, the publisher and a link. Citation styles such as APA or Chicago, locators like page numbers, and telling apart two works of one author in one year need a full CSL processor.

## Syntax highlighting

The Vite plugin highlights fenced code and [inline code with a language](docs#inline-code) with [twinkleplop][twinkleplop], with no setup. Every bundled language loads the first time a document has code.

Highlighted code is ordinary markup in your component, not `{@html}`. mdsvex escapes every `{` and `}` in it, so code is never read as a Svelte expression, whichever highlighter produced it.

### Themes

mdsvex adds no CSS. A twinkleplop theme is a stylesheet you import once, usually in your root layout:

```js
import '@twinkleplop/theme-github';
```

The themes are `@twinkleplop/theme-github`, `theme-atom-one`, `theme-ayu`, `theme-catppuccin`, `theme-material`, `theme-night-owl`, `theme-rose-pine` and `theme-solarized`. Each has three stylesheets:

- the package itself, light colours by default and dark ones under a `.dark` ancestor, for sites that toggle a `dark` class;
- `/light` and `/dark`, one variant each, for a fixed mode.

To follow the reader's system setting, import the light variant and add the dark one under a media query:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+layout.svelte -->
<script>
	import '@twinkleplop/theme-github/light';
	import '../app.css';

	let { children } = $props();
</script>

{@render children()}
```

```css
/* src/app.css */
@import '@twinkleplop/theme-github/dark' (prefers-color-scheme: dark);
```

A theme only colours tokens. It sets `--twp-background` without applying it, and leaves the block's layout and the classes that [fence meta](docs#fence-meta) and [code directives](docs#code-directives) add to you:

```css
pre.twinkleplop {
	padding: 1em;
	overflow-x: auto;
	background: var(--twp-background);
}

.twinkleplop .highlight,
.twinkleplop .highlighted-word {
	background: rgb(127 127 127 / 0.2);
}

.twinkleplop .diff-add {
	background: rgb(0 200 0 / 0.15);
}

.twinkleplop .diff-del {
	background: rgb(200 0 0 / 0.15);
}

.twinkleplop .ln {
	display: inline-block;
	width: 2em;
	margin-right: 1em;
	text-align: right;
	opacity: 0.5;
}

.has-focus .l:not(.focus) {
	opacity: 0.5;
}
```

A highlighted block renders as below. Each line is a `span.l`, each token a `span.tok` with its type as a class, and a line number a `span.ln`:

```html
<pre class="twinkleplop language-ts has-highlight" data-language="ts"><code><span class="l highlight"><span class="tok keyword">const</span> …</span></code></pre>
```

### Highlight options

`highlight` takes these options in `mdsvex()`:

```ts
interface HighlightOptions {
	languages?: Record<string, LanguageModule | HtmlHighlighter | string>;
	default_language?: string;
	on_unknown_language?: 'plain' | 'throw';
	line_numbers?: boolean | { start?: number };
	annotations?: AnnotationPlugin[] | false;
	render?: RenderOptions;
	parse_meta?: (raw: string, parsed: RenderOptions) => RenderOptions | void;
	twoslash?: boolean | { svelte?: boolean };
}
```

- `languages` adds languages and aliases, merged over the bundled ones. See [languages](docs#languages).
- `default_language` highlights fences that name no language.
- `on_unknown_language` decides what happens to a language mdsvex doesn't have. `'plain'`, the default, renders it as plain code and warns once per file and language. `'throw'` fails the build.
- `line_numbers` numbers every fence's lines, `{ start: 10 }` from 10. A fence's own meta overrides it.
- `annotations` lists the [code directives](docs#code-directives). Leave it out for the defaults, or pass `false` for none.
- `render` is twinkleplop's render options under every fence's own, such as `indent_guides`, `whitespace` or the `line` and `token` hooks. See [twinkleplop's render options](https://twinkleplop.pngwn.at/docs/render_options).
- `parse_meta` gets a fence's raw meta and the render options the [meta conventions](docs#fence-meta) set, and returns the render options to use, for house conventions of your own.
- `twoslash` turns on [twoslash](docs#twoslash).

`highlight` also takes a [function](docs#custom-highlighters), or `false` to render every block as a plain `<pre><code class="language-js">`.

### Languages

These languages are bundled: `bash`, `c`, `cpp`, `css`, `diff`, `dockerfile`, `dotenv`, `go`, `graphql`, `html`, `http`, `ini`, `javascript`, `json`, `jsonc`, `markdown`, `powershell`, `python`, `rust`, `shellsession`, `sql`, `svelte`, `toml`, `tsx`, `typescript` and `yaml`. Fences inside a `markdown` block are highlighted too.

These aliases are built in:

| alias | language |
| --- | --- |
| `js`, `mjs`, `cjs` | `javascript` |
| `ts`, `mts`, `cts` | `typescript` |
| `jsx` | `tsx` |
| `sh`, `shell`, `zsh` | `bash` |
| `console`, `shell-session` | `shellsession` |
| `yml` | `yaml` |
| `md` | `markdown` |
| `env` | `dotenv` |
| `patch` | `diff` |
| `py` | `python` |
| `rs` | `rust` |

A fence's language is looked up as written, then lowercased, so `Dockerfile` and `JSON` work. A language mdsvex doesn't have renders as plain code, in the same markup, so themes and line numbers still apply, with one warning per file. A fence with no language renders the same way, unless you set `default_language`.

`languages` adds to these. A string is an alias. A twinkleplop language package is passed as its module namespace:

```js
import * as zig from '@acme/twinkleplop-zig';

mdsvex({
	highlight: {
		languages: { zig, zg: 'zig', svx: 'markdown', vue: 'html' }
	}
});
```

A language can also be a function, `(code, render) => html`, that renders HTML itself. Code directives don't apply to it.

### Fence meta

The info string after the language takes the conventions of Shiki, VitePress and rehype-pretty-code:

| meta | effect |
| --- | --- |
| `{1,3-4}` | highlights lines 1, 3 and 4 with the `highlight` class |
| `{1,3-4}#id` | adds `data-highlighted-line-id="id"` to those lines |
| `/word/` | highlights every `word` with the `highlighted-word` class |
| `/word/2-3`, `/word/#id` | the second and third `word` only, or adds `data-chars-id="id"` |
| `:line-numbers`, `:line-numbers=10`, `:no-line-numbers` | turns line numbers on, from 10, or off |
| `showLineNumbers`, `showLineNumbers{10}` | turns line numbers on, from 10 |
| `[math.ts]`, `title="math.ts"` | a title |
| `caption="…"` | a caption |
| `twoslash` | uses [twoslash](docs#twoslash) |
| `src=./math.ts`, `src=./math.ts#L5-L20` | shows [a file](docs#code-from-a-file), or some of its lines |
| `run`, `run=client` | [runs the block](docs#code-that-runs) as a module, with the page or when asked |

````md
```ts title="math.ts" {2} :line-numbers
export function add(a: number, b: number) {
	return a + b;
}
```
````

A fence with a title or caption renders inside a `<figure class="twinkleplop-block">`, with a `<figcaption class="twinkleplop-title">` above the code and a `<figcaption class="twinkleplop-caption">` below it. A [replaced `pre`](docs#code-block-components) gets them as props instead. With `highlight: false` there's no figure, and the fence renders as a plain `<pre><code>`.

A line range past the end of the fence fails the build, so a stale range doesn't silently highlight nothing. The `language-` class carries only the language, never the rest of the meta.

A [parse plugin](docs#parse_plugins) reads the same conventions with `fence_meta`, from the info string a `code_fence` node has in its handler:

```js
import { fence_meta } from 'mdsvex/compile';

const titled = {
	code_fence: {
		parse(node) {
			const { lang, title, src, props } = fence_meta(node.info);
			// 'ts', 'math.ts', undefined, [['wrap', true]] for ```ts [math.ts] wrap
		}
	}
};
```

It gives the language, the rest as `meta`, the `title` and `caption`, what `src=` names, `run` for a fence that [runs](docs#code-that-runs), the `key=value` pairs and bare flags no convention claims as `props`, and every part as it was written as `parts`.

### Code from a file

`src=` in the meta of a fence shows a file in place of code written in the document, so the docs never drift from the code they describe. The fence itself stays empty.

````md
```ts src=./math.ts
```

```src=#lib/math.ts#L5-L20 title="math.ts"
```
````

The path resolves as an import from the document would: a relative path, a Vite alias, or a `#lib/…` subpath import. Write `./math.ts`, since a bare `math.ts` is a package name to an import. Quote a path with spaces, `src="./my file.ts"`.

A slice after the path shows some of the lines, counted from 1:

| slice | lines |
| --- | --- |
| `#L5` | line 5 |
| `#L5-L20` | lines 5 to 20 |
| `#L5-` | line 5 to the end |
| `#-L20` | the start to line 20 |

A fence with no language takes it from the file name, so `src=./math.ts` is highlighted as `ts` and `src=./Dockerfile` as `dockerfile`. A language you write wins. Everything else in the meta works as it does on any fence: line highlights count the lines shown, a title is still `[math.ts]` or `title=`, and `src=` sets none.

The file is highlighted like code written in the fence, and a [replaced `pre`](docs#code-block-components) gets `src` as a prop, as written. In dev, saving the file updates every page that shows it, and a watch build compiles those documents again.

These fail the build, each at the fence:

- the file does not resolve or cannot be read;
- the slice asks for lines the file does not have, so a stale range never silently shows the wrong code;
- the fence has code of its own, which would otherwise go missing.

`compile()` reads no files. Give it the text of each file in `sources`, keyed by the path as the fence has it, without the slice:

```js
compile(source, {
	sources: { './math.ts': 'export const a = 1;\n' }
});
```

A fence whose file is not in `sources` throws a `SourceError`, which has a `code` (`src_missing`, `src_slice`, `src_body` or `src_spec`), the `spec`, and a `line` and `column`. `sources: false` leaves `src=` unread, and the fence renders as written. The editor reads no files either, so it shows such a fence empty and reports only a fence that has code of its own.

### Code directives

A code directive is a marker in a comment, `[!verb]`, that styles part of the code and disappears from the output. A line that holds only markers is dropped.

```js
const config = {
	theme: 'light', // [!del]
	theme: 'dark', // [!add]
	lines: true // [!hl]
};
```

These are on by default:

| directive | class | for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[!hl]` | `highlight` | a highlighted line |
| `[!em]` | `emphasis` | drawing attention |
| `[!focus]` | `focus`, and `has-focus` on the `<pre>` | the lines to look at |
| `[!dim]` | `subdued` | fading lines out |
| `[!add]`, `[!del]`, `[!mod]` | `diff-add`, `diff-del`, `diff-mod` | diffs |
| `[!err]`, `[!warn]`, `[!info]` | `error`, `warning`, `info` | diagnostics |
| `[!eval]` | none | [live values](docs#live-code) |

With no arguments, a directive applies to its own line. `[!hl +2]` applies to the two lines below the marker, `[!hl :3...5]` to lines 3 to 5, and `[!hl =total]` to every `total` on the marker's line. [Twinkleplop's directive docs][twinkleplop-annotations] have every form. The `<pre>` also gets a `has-` class for each class inside it, such as `has-diff-add`.

`annotations` picks the directives. Content written for Shiki's transformers, `// [!code ++]`, works with `shiki_notation` added:

```js
import { default_annotations, shiki_notation } from 'mdsvex/highlight';

mdsvex({
	highlight: { annotations: [...default_annotations, shiki_notation()] }
});
```

`annotations: false` turns directives off, and markers stay in the code as written.

### Inline code

A code span that starts with ` #! ` and a language is highlighted:

```md
Call `#!ts add(1, 2)` to add numbers.
```

It renders as `<code class="twinkleplop twinkleplop-inline language-ts">`, with token spans inside and no ` #!ts `. A code span without ` #! ` stays a plain `<code>`. A [`code` replacement](docs#element-props) gets highlighted spans as `children`, and the language as a `lang` prop.

### Code block components

A [`pre` replacement](docs#custom-components) wraps the highlighted block. It gets these props:

| prop | value |
| --- | --- |
| `children` | the whole highlighted `<pre>` element |
| `lang` | the fence's language |
| `meta` | the rest of the info string, raw |
| `code` | the text a reader sees, without directive markers, with [live values](docs#live-code) in place |
| `title`, `caption` | from the [fence meta](docs#fence-meta), when it has them |
| `src` | the path a fence [takes its code from](docs#code-from-a-file), as written, slice included |
| `module` | the module of a fence marked [`run`](docs#code-that-runs) |
| `load` | a function that imports the module of a fence marked [`run=client`](docs#running-on-the-client) |
| meta props | each part of the meta that no convention claims |

`children` is the `<pre>` itself, not its `<code>`, because Svelte only keeps whitespace inside a `<pre>` it can see. Render it with `{@render children()}` inside your own markup, never inside another `<pre>`. A replaced `pre` gets no `<figure>`, so the title, caption and anything else around the code are up to the component.

A copy button with a filename:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Pre.svelte -->
<script>
	let { code, title, children } = $props();

	let copied = $state(false);

	async function copy() {
		await navigator.clipboard.writeText(code);
		copied = true;
		setTimeout(() => (copied = false), 1500);
	}
</script>

<div class="code">
	<div class="bar">
		{#if title}<span>{title}</span>{/if}
		<button onclick={copy}>{copied ? 'copied' : 'copy'}</button>
	</div>
	{@render children()}
</div>
```

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as pre } from './markdown/Pre.svelte';
```

Meta that no convention claims becomes props. `key="value"`, `key='value'` and `key=value` give a string, and a bare `key` gives `true`:

````md
```svelte title="App.svelte" playground height=300
<h1>Hello</h1>
```
````

That fence gives `playground={true}` and `height="300"`, as well as `title`. Expression values, `key={…}`, aren't read. A meta prop named `lang`, `meta`, `code`, `title`, `caption`, `class`, `children`, `src`, `module` or `load` is dropped with a warning. Meta props, `title` and `caption` don't depend on the highlighter, so a `pre` gets them with `highlight: false` too.

See the [code blocks playground example](/playground/code).

### Custom highlighters

`highlight` can be a function instead of options. It gets the code and what mdsvex knows about it, and returns HTML:

```ts
type Highlighter = (
	code: string,
	info: { lang: string; meta: string; inline: boolean; filename?: string }
) => string | null | undefined;
```

- `lang` is the fence's language, `""` when it names none, and `meta` the rest of the info string.
- `inline` is `true` for a [code span with ` #! `](docs#inline-code). Plain code spans never reach the highlighter.
- It returns a whole `<pre>` for a fence, or a `<code>` for inline code. `null` or `undefined` renders that code plain.
- It must be synchronous. Do any async setup before `mdsvex()` is called, with top-level `await` in your Vite config.
- It returns ordinary HTML. Don't escape it for Svelte, with `escapeSvelte` or anything else, and don't wrap it in `{@html}`. mdsvex escapes every brace in what you return, so a `{@html …}` wrapper would show up as text.

With [Shiki][shiki]:

```js
// vite.config.js
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { createHighlighter } from 'shiki';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

const shiki = await createHighlighter({
	themes: ['github-dark'],
	langs: ['javascript', 'typescript', 'svelte']
});

export default defineConfig({
	plugins: [
		mdsvex({
			highlight: (code, { lang, inline }) =>
				inline || !shiki.getLoadedLanguages().includes(lang)
					? null
					: shiki.codeToHtml(code, { lang, theme: 'github-dark' })
		}),
		sveltekit()
	]
});
```

Shiki's transformers go in the `codeToHtml` call, as they would anywhere else. A [`pre` replacement](docs#code-block-components) wraps a custom highlighter's output too, and gets the same props.

### Twoslash

Twoslash shows type information from the TypeScript compiler in your code. It is opt-in, as it starts a TypeScript environment. Install it:

```bash
npm i --save-dev @twinkleplop/twoslash
```

Turn it on, and import its styles next to your theme:

```js
mdsvex({ highlight: { twoslash: true } });
```

```js
import '@twinkleplop/twoslash/style.css';
```

Fences with `twoslash` in their meta then go through twoslash. `twoslash: true` covers `ts`, `js` and `tsx` fences. For Svelte fences too, install `@twinkleplop/twoslash-svelte` as well and use `twoslash: { svelte: true }`.

````md
```ts twoslash
const total = [1, 2, 3].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
//    ^?
```
````

Turning it on without the package installed is an error that names the package.

### Highlighting with `compile`

`compile` is synchronous and loads nothing, so it doesn't highlight unless you pass `highlight`. `mdsvex/highlight` builds the same configuration the plugin uses:

```js
import { compile } from 'mdsvex/compile';
import { create_highlight, load_default_languages } from 'mdsvex/highlight';

const highlight = create_highlight({
	languages: await load_default_languages()
});

const { code } = compile(source, { highlight, filename: 'post.svx' });
```

`create_highlight` takes the [options](docs#highlight-options) above, apart from `twoslash`, which takes highlighters by language. `load_default_languages` loads every bundled language once. In a browser, `language_loaders` loads them one at a time, so you only fetch what you use:

```js
import { create_highlight, language_loaders } from 'mdsvex/highlight';

const highlight = create_highlight({
	languages: {
		svelte: await language_loaders.svelte(),
		typescript: await language_loaders.typescript()
	}
});
```

`compile` also takes a [highlighter function](docs#custom-highlighters), or `false`.

## Math

A fenced code block with the language `math` is display math:

````md
```math
\int_0^\infty e^{-x^2}\,dx = \frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2}
```
````

The Vite plugin renders the LaTeX when it compiles the document, with [Temml][temml], into MathML. Browsers draw MathML themselves, so the page loads no JavaScript for math. The fence renders as:

```html
<div class="math math-display" translate="no">
	<math display="block" class="tml-display">…</math>
</div>
```

There is no `<pre>` or `<code>` around it, and the fence never reaches the highlighter, whatever [`highlight`](docs#highlight) is. Braces in the LaTeX need no escaping. mdsvex makes the output safe for Svelte, whichever renderer produced it. The `<div>` is there because MathML takes no `translate` attribute of its own, and it gives you something to style.

Inline math is a code span wrapped in dollars, on one line:

```md
Euler's identity, $`e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0`$, links five constants.
```

```html
<span class="math math-inline" translate="no"><math>…</math></span>
```

Both dollars have to touch the backticks. The LaTeX is raw, as in the fence, and there is no `<code>` around the result. It works wherever a code span does: in a heading, a list, a table cell or the text of a link. In a table cell write the LaTeX double bar as `\Vert`, since `\|` there is an escaped cell bar. GitHub and GitLab render the same source as math.

Temml loads the first time a document has math, so a site without math never loads it.

Only these two forms are math. A `latex` or `tex` fence is a code block that shows its source. `$…$` and `$$…$$` are plain text, so a price such as `$5` needs no escaping, and a `$` beside a code span that the other `$` doesn't follow is a `$` (`` $`x` `` is a dollar and inline code). [Inline code](docs#inline-code) with the hint, `` `#!math x^2` ``, is plain inline code.

Anything after `math` on the fence line is kept, and is reserved for later use. Nothing reads it today, apart from a [`math` component](docs#math-components).

### Math styles

mdsvex adds no CSS. Temml's stylesheet is in the `temml` package, so add it to your own dependencies and import the stylesheet once, usually in your root layout:

```bash
npm i --save-dev temml
```

```js
import 'temml/dist/Temml-Local.css';
```

`Temml-Local.css` uses a math font the reader already has. It asks for Cambria Math, then STIX Two Math, then Noto Sans Math, then the browser's own math font. The only font it loads is a 9 KB one for script capitals. Without the stylesheet the math still renders, but spacing and a few constructs differ between browsers.

The package also has `Temml-Latin-Modern.css`, `Temml-Asana.css`, `Temml-STIX2.css`, `Temml-Libertinus.css` and `Temml-NotoSans.css`, one for each web font. Each expects its font file beside it, and the package doesn't include those fonts. Copy the stylesheet and the font into your project, as [Temml's documentation describes][temml-fonts].

A wide equation can be wider than the page. Let it scroll:

```css
.math-display {
	overflow-x: auto;
}
```

### KaTeX

[KaTeX][katex] renders HTML that it lays out with its own fonts, with MathML beside it for screen readers. It looks the same in every browser, and it is several times more markup than MathML alone. Install it yourself and pick it:

```bash
npm i --save-dev katex
```

```js
mdsvex({ math: { renderer: 'katex' } });
```

```js
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';
```

KaTeX needs its stylesheet. Without it the reader sees every equation twice. Picking `'katex'` without the package installed is an error that names the package.

### Math options

`options` is passed to the renderer, [Temml's options][temml-options] or [KaTeX's][katex-options]:

```js
mdsvex({
	math: {
		options: {
			macros: { '\\RR': '\\mathbb{R}' }
		}
	}
});
```

mdsvex sets `displayMode` itself. It also sets `throwOnError`, so that LaTeX the renderer can't read fails the build. Pass `throwOnError: false` to draw the error in the page instead.

A definition made in a fence with `\gdef` ends with that fence. Documents compile in no fixed order, so a definition that leaked from one into the next would work on some builds and not on others. Put shared definitions in `macros`.

With Temml, `annotate: true` adds the LaTeX to the MathML as an annotation. KaTeX always adds it.

### Math errors

LaTeX the renderer can't read is a `MathError`. The message names the file and the line of the fence or of the inline math, and `line` and `column` point at the place in the LaTeX when the renderer says where it is, so Vite's error overlay opens on it:

```
Unexpected end of input in a macro argument, expected '}' at end of input: \frac{a}{ (`math` fence at src/routes/post.svx:12)
Unexpected end of input in a macro argument, expected '}' at end of input: \frac{a}{ (inline math at src/routes/post.svx:20)
```

`cause` is the renderer's own error.

### Math components

A `math` export in a [components module](docs#custom-components) replaces math:

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as math } from './markdown/Math.svelte';
```

It gets these props:

| prop | value |
| --- | --- |
| `children` | the rendered math, the same `<div class="math math-display">` or `<span class="math math-inline">` it renders as without a replacement |
| `code` | the LaTeX, as written |
| `display` | `true` for a fence, not passed for inline math |
| `meta` | the rest of the fence line, raw, when there is any |

The same component gets both kinds, so one that draws a block around the math does so only when `display` is set.

A button that copies the LaTeX of a fence:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Math.svelte -->
<script>
	let { code, display = false, children } = $props();
</script>

{#if display}
	<figure class="equation">
		{@render children()}
		<button onclick={() => navigator.clipboard.writeText(code)}>copy LaTeX</button>
	</figure>
{:else}
	{@render children()}
{/if}
```

A `pre` or `code` replacement never gets math, and a `math` replacement never gets code. With [`math: false`](docs#math) a `math` fence is a code block again, so it goes to `pre`, and inline math is inline code that shows the LaTeX, so it goes to `code`.

With [`component_mode: 'all'`](docs#component_mode-all), a `<math>` element you type yourself goes to the same component, with its attributes as props and no `code`. The MathML a renderer made is never replaced, in either mode.

See the [math playground example](/playground/math).

### Custom math renderers

`math` can be a function instead of options. It gets the LaTeX and returns markup, such as MathML, HTML or SVG:

```js
mdsvex({
	math: (latex, { display, meta, filename }) => render_somehow(latex, display)
});
```

`display` is `true` for a fence and `false` for inline math, and `meta` is the rest of the fence line, empty for inline math. It must be synchronous, and it should throw when it can't render, which mdsvex reports as a [`MathError`](docs#math-errors). It doesn't escape anything for Svelte. mdsvex does that, and puts the result in the `<div>` or the `<span>`.

### Math with `compile`

`compile` is synchronous and loads nothing, so a `math` fence is a code block and inline math is inline code unless you pass `math`. `load_math` from `mdsvex/math` imports the renderer and takes the same object as the plugin option:

```js
import { compile } from 'mdsvex/compile';
import { load_math } from 'mdsvex/math';

const math = await load_math({ options: { annotate: true } });

const { code } = compile(source, { math, filename: 'post.svx' });
```

Where you can't await, import the library yourself and pass it to `temml_math` or `katex_math`:

```js
import temml from 'temml';
import { temml_math } from 'mdsvex/math';

const math = temml_math(temml, { annotate: true });
```

`compile` also takes a [renderer function](docs#custom-math-renderers), or `false`.

The editor reads the option from the manifest the plugin writes, or from `math` in an `mdsvex.config.json`, and reports LaTeX that Temml can't read as you type. With KaTeX or a function it doesn't check the LaTeX.

## Footnotes

A footnote is a reference in the text and a definition anywhere in the document:

```md
A claim[^1], and a second one[^smith].

[^1]: The source, with a [link](https://example.com).
[^smith]: Smith, 2020.
```

The reference renders as a number that links to the note, and the notes are collected at the end of the document, in the order they are first referred to:

```html
<p>A claim<sup><a href="#fn-1" id="fnref-1" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>, and a second one<sup><a href="#fn-smith" id="fnref-2" role="doc-noteref">2</a></sup>.</p>

<section class="footnotes" role="doc-endnotes" aria-label="Footnotes">
<ol>
<li id="fn-1"><p>The source, with a <a href="https://example.com">link</a>. <a href="#fnref-1" role="doc-backlink" aria-label="Back to reference 1">&#8617;</a></p></li>
<li id="fn-smith"><p>Smith, 2020. <a href="#fnref-2" role="doc-backlink" aria-label="Back to reference 2">&#8617;</a></p></li>
</ol>
</section>
```

- **A label** is any run of characters without whitespace, brackets or `^`. Labels match exactly, case included. The number a reader sees comes from the order of the references, not from the label, so `[^smith]` is as good a label as `[^2]`.
- **A definition** is a line that starts with `[^label]:` and has text after it. It may hold links, emphasis, code and references to other notes. It can be written anywhere: under the paragraph that cites it, at the end of the document, inside a block quote or a list item. Wherever it is, it renders in the section at the end.
- **A longer note** continues as a list item does. After a blank line, a block indented by four spaces belongs to the note, and the first line that is not blank and not indented ends it:

  ```md
  [^1]: The source.

      A second paragraph, and a list:

      - one
      - two

  Not part of the note.
  ```
- **Referring to a note twice** gives both references the same number, and the note a link back to each.
- **The id of a note** is `fn-` and its label, so a link to a note survives a renumbering. The id of a reference is `fnref-` and its number. With [`heading_ids`](docs#heading_ids), a heading whose slug would be one of these gets a suffix.
- **mdsvex adds no CSS** and no heading. The section has the `footnotes` class and an accessible name. The arrow of a back link is followed by a variation selector, so it is drawn as text and not as an emoji.

A reference is read by its syntax alone, before any definition is known. So a `[^…]` in prose that is not a footnote, such as the regular expression class `[^abc]`, has to be escaped or put in code:

```md
The class \[^abc] matches, and so does `[^abc]`.
```

### Footnote errors

A reference with no definition and a label defined twice fail the build with a `FootnoteError`. It has the `code` (`footnote_undefined` or `footnote_duplicate`), the `label`, and the `line` and `column` of the reference or of the second definition, and `problems` lists every one in the document:

```
the footnote reference [^nope] has no definition, add `[^nope]: text`, or escape the bracket as `\[^nope]` if it is not a footnote (src/routes/post.svx:12)
```

A definition that nothing refers to is not rendered, and the compile warns with the code `footnote_unused`.

### Footnote options

```js
mdsvex({
	footnotes: {
		label: 'Notes',
		back_label: 'Back to note {n}'
	}
});
```

`label` names the section and `back_label` each link back, where `{n}` is the number of the reference. `strict: false` turns the two errors into warnings: a reference with no definition is numbered and linked all the same, and the first definition of a label is the one that renders. That suits a preview of a document that is still being written.

### Footnote components

Two exports of a [components module](docs#custom-components) replace footnotes:

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as footnote } from './markdown/Footnote.svelte';
export { default as footnotes } from './markdown/Footnotes.svelte';
```

`footnote` replaces a reference. It gets these props:

| prop | value |
| --- | --- |
| `children` | the link it renders as without a replacement, the `<sup>` |
| `note` | a snippet of what the definition holds |
| `label` | the label, as written |
| `number` | its number |
| `href` | the link to the note, `#fn-` and the label |
| `id` | the id of the reference, `fnref-` and the number |

`note` is what a side note or a hover card needs. A note holds blocks, and HTML allows no block inside the paragraph a reference is in, so draw it once the page is in the browser, as this card does on hover, or somewhere outside the paragraph:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Footnote.svelte -->
<script>
	let { children, note } = $props();

	let open = $state(false);
</script>

<span
	class="footnote"
	role="group"
	onpointerenter={() => (open = true)}
	onpointerleave={() => (open = false)}
>
	{@render children()}{#if open}<span class="card">{@render note()}</span>{/if}
</span>
```

`footnotes` replaces the section and gets the `<ol>` of notes as its `children`:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Footnotes.svelte -->
<script>
	let { children } = $props();
</script>

<aside class="notes" aria-label="Notes">
	<h2>Notes</h2>
	{@render children()}
</aside>
```

The links and the list that mdsvex writes for footnotes are not markdown, so `a`, `sup`, `ol` and `li` replacements never get them. What a note holds is markdown, so its paragraphs and links are replaced as anywhere else. A reference inside a `note` snippet gets no snippet of its own.

See the [footnotes playground example](/playground/footnotes).

### Footnotes while streaming

`compile` knows the whole document, so it can number the notes and move them. The streaming renderer does not: a reference shows its label as a superscript, and a definition renders where it is written, behind the same label.

## Live code

Braces in code blocks are text. To put a value from the document into a code block, mark it live with an `[!eval]` comment:

````svx
<script>
	let install_command = 'pnpm add';
</script>

```sh
{install_command} @pkg/my-pkg # [!eval]
```
````

Every top-level `{...}` group on the marker's line becomes a Svelte expression, so readers see `pnpm add @pkg/my-pkg`. The marker itself is removed, and a line that holds only a marker is dropped.

To make one group live and leave the rest as text, name it:

````svx
```js
function my_func() {
	console.log({ a: {some_val} }) // [!eval ="{some_val}"]
}
```
````

Here the outer braces stay text and `{some_val}` is live.

`[!eval]` is a [code directive](docs#code-directives), so it is written in a comment of the fence's language: `//` or `/* */` in JavaScript, `#` in shell, `<!-- -->` in HTML and Svelte. It needs syntax highlighting, which the Vite plugin turns on by default.

### Marker forms

`[!eval]` takes the same arguments as the other code directives:

| marker | live groups |
| --- | --- |
| `[!eval]` | every top-level `{...}` on the marker's line |
| `[!eval +N]` | every top-level `{...}` on the N lines below |
| `[!eval :N..M]`, `[!eval :N...M]` | every top-level `{...}` on lines N to M, exclusive or inclusive |
| `[!eval ="{x}"]` | each `{x}` on the marker's line |
| `[!eval ="{x}" :*]` | each `{x}` in the code block |
| `[!eval a...b]` | the top-level groups between the words `a` and `b` on the line |
| `[!eval a...]` and `[!eval ...b]` | the top-level groups from `a` on one line to `b` on a later one |

A marker whose range holds no group warns.

### What can be live

- **Values come from the document**: its `<script>`, and its frontmatter as `metadata`.
- **A value renders as text.** Svelte escapes it, so markup in it shows as written. Write `{@html value}` to render markup.
- **Only expression tags**, `{expr}` and `{@html expr}`. Block tags such as `{#if}` and `{:else}`, and tags such as `{@const}`, fail the build.
- **One line each.** A group that runs onto the next line fails the build.
- **Strings don't count.** Braces inside strings and template literals in a group are skipped, so `{a ? "}" : b}` is one group. A group that never closes fails the build with its line and column.
- **Empty braces stay text**, so `{}` is never live.

A live group keeps the colour of the token around it, so `"{base_url}/api"` stays string-coloured.

### The `eval` flag

`eval` in a fence's meta means `[!eval]` on every line:

````svx
```sh eval
{install_command} @pkg/my-pkg
{run_command} dev
```
````

It works on any fence, including languages without comments, such as `json`. A fence with no language takes it as its language, ```` ```eval ````.

The flag makes every top-level group in the block live. That suits commands, config and other brace-light code. In JavaScript or CSS every object literal and block would become an expression, so use markers there.

### Copying live code

A [replaced `pre`](docs#code-block-components) gets the code as its `code` prop, with live values in place. The prop is a template literal, such as ``code={`${install_command} @pkg/my-pkg`}``, so a copy button copies what the reader sees.

A [custom highlighter](docs#custom-highlighters) doesn't support live code yet. A fence with the `eval` flag warns and its braces stay text.

## Code that runs

A fence marked `run` is shown as code and is also a module the page imports. The component that [replaces `pre`](docs#code-block-components) gets that module as a prop and decides what running means: render a component, call an export, print a result.

````svx
```svelte run
<script>
	let count = $state(0);
</script>

<button onclick={() => count++}>clicked {count}</button>
```
````

The block is a real module in the Vite graph, not a string that is evaluated. TypeScript is compiled, a Svelte block is compiled by vite-plugin-svelte, and its styles are scoped as in any component. Imports in the block resolve as they would in the document, so `./Button.svelte` is a file beside it, and aliases and `#lib/…` work. The server renders it with the page, and a content security policy has nothing to object to.

A block can't see the document's `<script>`. It is a module of its own, as a file would be, so share values by importing them in both, or let your component pass props to what the block exports.

See the [code that runs playground example](/playground/run).

### A component for `pre`

mdsvex hands over the module and nothing else, so a document with a block that runs needs a `pre` replacement, from a [`components` module](docs#the-components-option) or a [template](docs#replacing-elements-in-a-template). Without one the build fails at the fence.

This one renders a Svelte block as a component and calls the default export of a script block, each above its code:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Pre.svelte -->
<script>
	let { lang, module, load, children } = $props();

	let loaded = $state();
	const ran = $derived(module ?? loaded);
	const Demo = $derived(lang === 'svelte' ? ran?.default : undefined);
	const result = $derived(
		lang !== 'svelte' && typeof ran?.default === 'function'
			? ran.default()
			: undefined
	);
</script>

{#if Demo}
	<div class="demo"><Demo /></div>
{:else if result !== undefined}
	<output class="demo">{String(result)}</output>
{:else if load}
	<button onclick={async () => (loaded = await load())}>run</button>
{/if}
{@render children()}
```

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as pre } from './markdown/Pre.svelte';
```

````svx
```ts run
import { format } from './dates';

export default () => format(new Date(2026, 0, 1));
```
````

`module` is the namespace of the block, so `module.default` is its default export and its named exports are beside it. A block without the flag gets no `module`, so the same component serves every fence.

Layout around a demo is a job for a [directive](docs#directives): the `pre` inside it renders the demo, and the directive's component puts a frame, a title or tabs around it.

````svx
:::Example[A counter]

```svelte run
<button>…</button>
```

:::
````

### Languages that run

`js`, `ts`, `jsx`, `tsx` and `svelte` blocks run, as do `javascript` and `typescript`. The language picks the extension of the module, which is how Vite knows what to do with it. `run` on a block in any other language, or on one with no language, fails the build.

### Running on the client

A block that touches `window` when it loads can't render on the server, and a heavy demo below the fold is better fetched late. `run=client` leaves the module out of the page and hands the component a function that imports it:

````svx
```js run=client
export default () => window.innerWidth;
```
````

The component gets `load` in place of `module`. Call it from an event handler or an effect, and it resolves to the same namespace `module` would hold. The block becomes a chunk of its own in a build.

### Running a file

With [`src=`](docs#code-from-a-file) a block shows a file, and with `run` as well the page imports that file. The component gets `src` and `module` both.

````svx
```svelte src=./Counter.svelte run
```
````

The module is the whole file, also when a slice shows only some of its lines.

### Editing a block

The id of a block's module is a hash of its language and code. In dev, an edit to one block changes only that block's import, so the page updates and every other block keeps the module it has: its top-level code doesn't run again and nothing it fetched is fetched again. Two blocks with the same code share one module.

An error in a block, at build time or in the browser, points at its line of the document.

### When a block can't run

These fail the build, each at the fence:

- no component replaces `pre`;
- the language is not one that runs;
- the block is also [live code](docs#live-code), with the `eval` flag or an `[!eval]` marker, since braces mean different things in each;
- `run=` has a value other than `client`.

### Running with `compile`

`compile()` emits the import, but it has no bundler to serve the module from, so by default a block that runs throws a `RunError`. Say how the block is imported with `snippets`:

```js
const { code, snippets } = compile(source, {
	filename: 'guide.md',
	snippets: true
});
```

`true` imports each block as a sibling of `filename`, `./guide.md.snippet-<hash>.<extension>`, the id the Vite plugin uses. A function gets the snippet and returns the specifier. Either way the result has `snippets`, one for each module, with its `hash`, `lang`, `extension`, `code` and the `line` of the document the code starts on, for your bundler's resolve and load hooks to serve. `snippets: false` runs no block: the flag is taken out of the meta and the fence is only shown.

A `RunError` has a `code` (`run_module`, `run_pre`, `run_lang`, `run_eval` or `run_value`) and a `line` and `column`. A block with `src=` imports its file, so it needs no `snippets`.

The editor shows a block that runs as code, and reports one the build would reject at its fence. It doesn't check the code as a module yet.

## Without Vite

The Vite plugin reads files, finds the names your modules export, serves modules that don't exist on disk and reloads pages as you edit. [`compile`](docs#compile) does none of that. It takes text and returns Svelte source, never touches the filesystem and runs in a browser, a worker or Node. Everything below is what a build tool, a playground or an editor that isn't Vite supplies to get the same output.

Import the compiler from `mdsvex/compile`, highlighting from `mdsvex/highlight` and math from `mdsvex/math`. None of them loads Vite or a Node builtin, and neither does `mdsvex/host`, which holds the pieces of the plugin a host needs beside the compiler.

### What a host supplies

| feature | with `compile` |
| --- | --- |
| markdown, frontmatter, `metadata` | nothing |
| footnotes, smart punctuation, heading ids, `unwrap_images` | the option |
| [highlighting](docs#highlighting-with-compile) | a highlighter from `mdsvex/highlight`, with its languages loaded up front |
| [math](docs#math-with-compile) | a renderer from `load_math` in `mdsvex/math` |
| [images as imports](docs#import_images) | `import_images: true`, then a bundler that imports images |
| [links](docs#links) | `routes`, and `base` as a string or `{ import, from }` |
| [code from a file](docs#code-from-a-file) | the text of each file in `sources`, or `read` and `resolve` with [`compile_async`](docs#compile_async) |
| [code that runs](docs#running-with-compile) | `snippets`, then serving each of `result.snippets` as a module |
| templates, components, directives | each module as its specifier and the names it exports, see below |
| plugins | `plugins`, as for the Vite plugin, and [`compile_async`](docs#compile_async) for one that [defers work](docs#deferred-work) |
| plugins that [load files](docs#loading-files) | `read` and `resolve`, with `compile_async` |
| rebuilding when a file changes | watching `dependencies` on the result, see [below](docs#files-and-plugins-that-wait) |
| [plugin data](docs#plugin-data) | nothing, it is `data` on the result |
| a template for one import | the `template` option of that compile, in place of `?template=` |

### Finding the names a module exports

`compile` takes templates, components and directives as a specifier and the names that module exports (see [`compile`](docs#compile)), and writes the specifier into the output as given. The Vite plugin finds the names by reading each module's exports without running it. `read_exports` from `mdsvex/host` does the same for you, with the TypeScript stripper you already have:

```js
import { read_exports } from 'mdsvex/host';
import ts from 'typescript';

const strip = (code) =>
	ts.transpileModule(code, { compilerOptions: { target: ts.ScriptTarget.ESNext } }).outputText;

const { names, stars, namespaces } = read_exports(source, 'svelte', strip);
```

For a `.svelte` file it reads the `<script module>`. Without a stripper it reads the code as JavaScript. `stars` lists the `export * from` specifiers whose names it can't see, and `namespaces` the `export * as name from` ones, which is how a module [declares its directives](docs#declaring-directives).

`names` includes `default`. Leave it out for a template, whose default export is the template itself. For a [document used as a template](docs#documents-as-templates), read the exports of its compiled `code` and leave out `metadata` as well.

### Virtual ids, `?metadata` and `?data`

The Vite plugin writes `mdsvex:components`, `mdsvex:template/<name>` and the other `mdsvex:*` ids in place of real paths and resolves them itself. `compile` writes whatever specifier you give it, so a host can use real paths and skip this. To match the plugin's output, `mdsvex/host` exports the prefixes (`COMPONENTS_ID`, `DIRECTIVES_ID`, `TEMPLATE_ID`, `TEMPLATE_DIRECTIVES_ID`, `PLUGIN_ID`, `PLUGIN_COMPONENTS_ID`), and `manifest_resolver(manifest)` returns the file an id and an imported name stand for.

A [`?metadata`](docs#frontmatter) or [`?data`](docs#plugin-data) import loads a module made from the document. `metadata_module(source, parse)` builds the first from the text of the document, with your [frontmatter parser](docs#custom-frontmatter-parsers) or `undefined`, and `data_module(result.metadata, result.data)` the second from a compile of it.

### The editor manifest

The editor reads `node_modules/.mdsvex/manifest.json`, which the Vite plugin writes, or else `mdsvex.config.json`, which can't hold functions. A host can write the manifest itself. `queue_manifest(build, output)` writes it a little after the last change and only when it changed, `manifest_json` gives the exact text the plugin writes, and `MANIFEST_PATH` is where it goes.

### Vite only

These need a dev server or Vite's config, so they stay with the plugin:

- **`links.base: true`**, which reads Vite's `base`. Give `compile` the base as a string, or as `{ import, from }` for a base known when the page renders.
- **The checks on `?metadata` and `?data` imports.** In dev the plugin serves a document only when Vite would serve the file, so a request can't read files outside the project.
- **Source maps back to markdown.** `compile` with `sourcemap: true` maps its own output, and `mappings_to_v3` from `@mdsvex/render/sourcemap` turns that into a v3 map. Chaining it with the Svelte compiler's map is up to your bundler.
- **`?template=`** on an import. Use the `template` option of the compile.
- **Hot update** of documents, of the files a fence shows and of template and component modules. A host that rebuilds watches `dependencies` instead, see below.

### Files and plugins that wait

`compile` reads nothing and waits for nothing. There are two ways to give a document the files it shows and the work its plugins defer:

- **[`compile_async`](docs#compile_async)** does it in one call. Pass `read` and `resolve` and it reads the files fences show with `src=` and what plugins load, then waits for deferred work. Use it for a build where the host can read files, in Node or a worker with a virtual filesystem.
- **[Compiling in steps](docs#compiling-in-steps)** hands the waiting to you: `parse`, then `settle` for deferred work, then read each of `parsed.needs` yourself and `render`. Use it when files arrive another way, such as a fetch or an editor's open buffers, or to compile other documents while one waits. The playground compiles this way.

The result's `dependencies` are absolute paths: what plugins read or named, and, when `compile_async` read them, the files fences show and the module of the template. Compiling in steps, you know the files you read for `needs` yourself. To rebuild as the Vite plugin does in dev, watch those files, and when one changes call `session.invalidate([path])` on your `CompilerSession`, so what [`ctx.cached`](docs#loading-files) kept from it goes, then compile the documents that list it again. With `compile_async`, pass that session as its third argument.

## Migrating from mdsvex 0.x

Layouts are now [templates](docs#templates-1). The idea is the same, a component that wraps documents and replaces their elements, with new names and Svelte 5 components.

### Run the migrator

`@mdsvex/migrate` rewrites your config and documents, and flags what it can't rewrite:

```bash
npm i --save-dev @mdsvex/migrate@next
```

It is a library of functions. A short script runs them over a project. Commit your work first, then set `config` to the file that calls `mdsvex()`, `layouts` to your layout components, and `options` to what your documents were written for:

```js
// migrate.js
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync, globSync } from 'node:fs';
import { migrate, migrate_config, check_template } from '@mdsvex/migrate';

const config = 'svelte.config.js';
const layouts = ['src/lib/layouts/Blog.svelte'];
// smartypants: your old smartypants option, true if you never set it
// math: true if you used remark-math
const options = { smartypants: true, math: false };

function report(file, notes) {
	for (const note of notes) console.log(`${file}:${note.line}: ${note.message}`);
}

const { code, notes } = migrate_config(readFileSync(config, 'utf8'));
writeFileSync(config, code);
report(config, notes);

for (const file of globSync('src/**/*.{svx,md}'))
	writeFileSync(file, migrate(readFileSync(file, 'utf8'), options));

for (const file of layouts) report(file, check_template(readFileSync(file, 'utf8')));
```

```bash
node migrate.js
```

- `migrate_config` rewrites the options of a JavaScript or TypeScript config: `layout` into `templates`, `highlight`, `smartypants` and `extension`. It returns the new `code` and a list of `notes`, things to check by hand, each with a `line` and a `message`. Every entry of `remarkPlugins` and `rehypePlugins` gets a note that says [what replaces it](docs#coming-from-remark-and-rehype-plugins).
- `migrate` converts a document to mdsvex's [markdown](docs#markdown) and returns it. 0.x read CommonMark, where `*text*` is emphasis and a `===` underline makes a heading, so every document needs it. Svelte in the document is kept as written, and the `layout:` key of the frontmatter becomes `template:`.
- `check_template` lists what a layout component needs changed. It rewrites nothing.

Read the diff before you commit it. `migrate` writes each document out again from what it parsed, so list markers, table padding and blank lines come out in one style.

The options stay where they were. SvelteKit 3 has no `svelte.config.js`, so move the `mdsvex()` call from `preprocess` into the `plugins` of `vite.config.js`, as in [SvelteKit](docs#sveltekit). mdsvex registers its extensions, so they don't need to move with it.

For a document that is already mdsvex's markdown, `migrate_frontmatter` renames the `layout:` key and changes nothing else.

### What changes

| mdsvex 0.x | now |
| --- | --- |
| `layout: './src/Layout.svelte'` | `templates: { default: './src/Layout.svelte' }` |
| `layout: { _: …, blog: … }` | `templates: { default: …, blog: … }` |
| a named layout applied to documents in a folder of the same name | `select_template`, which the migrator writes for you as a note |
| frontmatter `layout: blog`, `layout: false` | `template: blog`, `template: false` |
| frontmatter `layout: _` | `template: default` |
| `layoutPropForwarding` | always on, the migrator flags it |
| `<slot />` in a layout | `{@render children()}` |
| `export let title` in a layout | `let { title } = $props()` |
| `<script context="module">` exports | `<script module>` exports |
| `{title}` in a document | `{metadata.title}` |
| `extension: '.md'` | `extensions: ['.md']` |
| `frontmatter: { marker, type }` | gone, the fences are always `---` and [`frontmatter.parse`](docs#custom-frontmatter-parsers) reads what is between them |
| `rel="nofollow"` on every link to another site | no `rel`. `external_links({ rel: ['nofollow'] })` from [`@mdsvex/plugin-external-links`](docs#external-links) keeps it |
| `smartypants`, on by default | [`smart_punctuation`](docs#smart_punctuation), off by default, the migrator turns it on |
| `remarkPlugins`, `rehypePlugins` | gone. Most of what they did is built in or a first-party plugin, see [coming from remark and rehype plugins](docs#coming-from-remark-and-rehype-plugins) |
| CommonMark documents | mdsvex's own [markdown](docs#markdown), the migrator converts them |
| ```` ```js file=./a.js#L1-L5 ````, from `remark-code-import` | [`src=`](docs#code-from-a-file), the migrator renames `file=` on a fence with no code |
| `$x$` and `$$x$$`, from `remark-math` | [math](docs#math-1), the migrator rewrites them when you pass `math: true` |
| `![alt](./a.png)` served from the page's url | imported from beside the document, write `/a.png` for a file in `static`, or set [`import_images: false`](docs#import_images) |

The migrator handles the `layout`, `smartypants` and `extension` rows and converts the documents. It flags `<slot />`, `layoutPropForwarding`, the `frontmatter` options, the missing `nofollow` and every entry of `remarkPlugins` and `rehypePlugins`, each with what replaces it. The other rows are yours to change.

0.x matched named layouts to folders. Templates aren't matched by folder, so for named layouts the notes include a `select_template` to paste into your config:

```js
select_template: (id) => (id.includes('/blog/') ? 'blog' : undefined),
```

### `layout:` in frontmatter

mdsvex doesn't read a `layout:` key any more, and there is no alias for it. The migrator renames it to `template:`. A document you haven't migrated keeps `layout:` as ordinary metadata, and gets the `default` template, or the one `select_template` picks.

### Layout components

A layout needs two changes to work as a template:

```svelte
<!-- before -->
<script>
	export let title;
</script>

<h1>{title}</h1>
<slot />
```

```svelte
<!-- after -->
<script>
	let { title, children } = $props();
</script>

<h1>{title}</h1>
{@render children()}
```

`check_template` finds `<slot />`, `$$props`, `$$restProps` and `{...$$props}`. [`npx sv migrate svelte-5`][sv-migrate] migrates the rest of the component. Element replacements stay in the module script, now `<script module>`, and keep working.

### Syntax highlighting in 0.x

0.x highlighted with Prism, through a `highlight.highlighter` that could be async and returned `{@html}`. Now twinkleplop highlights by default, and mdsvex escapes whatever a highlighter returns.

| mdsvex 0.x | now |
| --- | --- |
| `highlight: false` | `highlight: false` |
| `highlight: { alias: { x: 'y' } }` | `highlight: { languages: { x: 'y' } }` |
| `highlight: { highlighter(code, lang, meta, filename) }`, sync or async | `highlight: (code, { lang, meta, inline, filename }) => html`, sync only |
| a highlighter returns `` {@html `…`} `` | a highlighter returns plain HTML |
| `highlight.optimise` | gone, highlighted code is always markup |
| `escapeSvelte` | gone, mdsvex escapes braces itself |
| Prism and `prism-svelte` | twinkleplop |
| a Prism theme you add by hand | a [twinkleplop theme](docs#themes) you import |
| a `pre` replacement doesn't apply to code blocks | it does, see [code block components](docs#code-block-components) |

`migrate_config` renames `alias` to `languages` and removes `optimise`. Alias targets are now twinkleplop's [languages](docs#languages), so it flags Prism names twinkleplop doesn't have, such as `markup`. It can't rewrite a `highlighter`, so it leaves a `// TODO(mdsvex-migrate)` comment above it, and it flags every `escapeSvelte` import.

A highlighter changes in three ways. It takes an object as its second argument, it returns HTML without escaping it, and it can't be async. A Shiki highlighter goes from this:

```js
import { escapeSvelte } from 'mdsvex';

const shiki = await createHighlighter({ themes: ['github-dark'], langs: ['ts'] });

const config = {
	highlight: {
		highlighter: async (code, lang = 'text') => {
			const html = escapeSvelte(shiki.codeToHtml(code, { lang, theme: 'github-dark' }));
			return `{@html \`${html}\` }`;
		}
	}
};
```

to this:

```js
const shiki = await createHighlighter({ themes: ['github-dark'], langs: ['ts'] });

mdsvex({
	highlight: (code, { lang, inline }) =>
		inline ? null : shiki.codeToHtml(code, { lang: lang || 'text', theme: 'github-dark' })
});
```

Or drop the highlighter and use the default. Either way, import a theme: highlighted code has no colours without one.

### Smart punctuation in 0.x

0.x curled quotes and made dashes and ellipses in every document unless you set `smartypants: false`. Now [`smart_punctuation`](docs#smart_punctuation) does that, and it is off unless you turn it on. `migrate_config` turns it on for a config that relied on the old default, so your pages keep their typography:

| mdsvex 0.x | now |
| --- | --- |
| no `smartypants` option | `smart_punctuation: true` is added |
| `smartypants: true` | `smart_punctuation: true` |
| `smartypants: false` | removed, off is the default |
| `smartypants: { quotes, ellipses }` | `smart_punctuation: { quotes, ellipses }` |
| `dashes: true`, the default: `--` is an em dash, `---` is left alone | `--` is an en dash and `---` an em dash |
| `dashes: 'oldschool'` | `dashes: true`, which now means the same |
| `dashes: 'inverted'` | `dashes: true`, and the documents are rewritten |
| `backticks`, which curled <code>&#96;&#96;quoted''</code> | gone, the documents are rewritten |

The dashes are the one change that shows. With the 0.x default, `--` was an em dash. It is an en dash now, as it is in pandoc, cmark and LaTeX. So that pages render what they did, pass your old `smartypants` value to `migrate` when it converts your documents, `true` if you never set it, as the script under [run the migrator](docs#run-the-migrator) does:

```js
migrate(source, { smartypants: true });
```

It turns each `--` in text into `---`, keeps a `---` that 0.x left alone as `\---`, swaps the two for `'inverted'`, and turns <code>&#96;&#96;quoted''</code> into `"quoted"`. Code, urls and attributes are not touched. The notes from `migrate_config` say which of this applies to your config.

Three things 0.x changed are left alone now, because they are not prose: a string inside a Svelte expression, an attribute such as `--css-var="x"` on a component, and the text of an autolinked url. And `--name` after a space is read as a command line flag, where 0.x gave it a dash.

## Coming from remark and rehype plugins

mdsvex 0.x ran remark and rehype, and took their plugins in `remarkPlugins` and `rehypePlugins`. mdsvex now has its own parser, so those plugins don't run and the two options are gone. Most of what they were used for is built in, an option, or a first-party plugin. Find each plugin you used below.

`migrate_config` from the [migrator](docs#run-the-migrator) leaves a note for every plugin it finds in your config, with the same answer.

### Built into the markdown

| plugin | now | how |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `remark-gfm`, `remark-footnotes` | built in, apart from one thing | [tables](docs#tables), `~~strikethrough~~`, [task lists](docs#task-lists) and [footnotes](docs#footnotes-1) work as written. A bare url is not linked: the migrator writes each one as `<https://example.com>`, and `references({ linkify: true })` from [`@mdsvex/plugin-references`](docs#references) links them all |
| `remark-frontmatter`, `remark-parse-yaml` | built in | [frontmatter](docs#frontmatter-1) is the `metadata` export, and [`frontmatter.parse`](docs#custom-frontmatter-parsers) takes TOML or full YAML |
| `remark-directive`, `remark-admonitions`, `remark-containers`, `remark-custom-blocks` | built in | [directives](docs#directives): `:::note` renders your `note` component |
| `remark-sub-super`, `remark-supersub` | built in | `H~2~O` and `x^2^` |
| `remark-extended-table`, `rehype-merge-cells` | built in | a `>` or `^` cell [merges](docs#tables), and `\|\|` makes header columns |
| `remark-math` with `rehype-katex`, `rehype-katex-svelte` or `rehype-mathjax` | built in | a [`math` fence and `` $`x`$ ``](docs#math-1), rendered by Temml or, with `math: { renderer: 'katex' }`, KaTeX. For MathJax pass a [function](docs#custom-math-renderers). The migrator rewrites `$x$` when you pass `math: true` |
| `remark-code-import` | built in | [`src=./file.ts#L5-L20`](docs#code-from-a-file) on a fence, the migrator renames `file=` |
| `remark-breaks` | nothing, on purpose | a line end is a space. End a line with a backslash for a break |
| `rehype-raw` | not needed | HTML in a document is always real markup |

### Now an option

| plugin | now | how |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `remark-smartypants`, and the `smartypants` option | an option | [`smart_punctuation: true`](docs#smart_punctuation), off by default |
| `rehype-slug`, `remark-slug` | an option | [`heading_ids: true`](docs#heading_ids), which the migrator adds |
| `remark-unwrap-images`, `rehype-unwrap-images` | an option | [`unwrap_images: true`](docs#unwrap_images), which the migrator adds |
| `mdsvex-relative-images`, `remark-embed-images` | on by default | a relative image is [imported](docs#import_images), so Vite hashes it |
| `remark-link-rewrite`, `rehype-urls`, `remark-prepend-url`, `remark-relative-links` | an option | [`links`](docs#links) for links between documents and the base path, [`rewrite`](docs#rewrite) for anything else |
| `rehype-highlight`, `remark-highlight.js`, `rehype-prism-plus`, `remark-prism`, `@shikijs/rehype`, `rehype-pretty-code` | on by default | [highlighting](docs#syntax-highlighting) needs a theme and no setup. [Fence meta](docs#fence-meta) reads `{1,3-4}`, `/word/` and `showLineNumbers` as they do. For Shiki itself pass a [function](docs#custom-highlighters) |
| `rehype-code-titles`, `remark-code-titles`, `remark-flexible-code-titles` | built in | `title="math.ts"` or `[math.ts]` in the [fence meta](docs#fence-meta) |
| `rehype-twoslash` | an option | [`highlight: { twoslash: true }`](docs#twoslash) |

### First-party plugins

| plugin | now | how |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `rehype-autolink-headings`, `remark-autolink-headings` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-anchors`](docs#anchors) | `anchors()`, with `heading_ids: true` |
| `rehype-external-links`, `remark-external-links` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-external-links`](docs#external-links) | `external_links({ target: '_blank' })`. 0.x ran this plugin itself, with `rel="nofollow"`, see [what changes](docs#what-changes) |
| `remark-toc`, `rehype-toc`, `@stefanprobst/rehype-extract-toc`, `@vcarl/remark-headings` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-toc`](docs#table-of-contents) | `toc()` leaves the headings as data, and `::toc` lists them in the page |
| `remark-reading-time` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-page-data`](docs#page-data) | `plugin_data.page.minutes` and `words` |
| `rehype-infer-description-meta`, `rehype-infer-title-meta`, `rehype-truncate` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-page-data`](docs#page-data) | `plugin_data.page.excerpt` and `title`, as text |
| `rehype-code-group` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-tabs`](docs#tabs) | `:::tabs` around the fences |
| `remark-emoji`, `remark-gemoji` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-emoji`](docs#emoji) | `emoji()` turns `:tada:` into the emoji, with GitHub's names |
| `remark-github`, `remark-mentions`, `remark-ping` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-references`](docs#references) | `references({ repo: 'you/site' })` links `@user`, `#123` and commits |
| `remark-linkify-regex`, autolink literals | [`@mdsvex/plugin-references`](docs#references) | `references({ linkify: true })` links bare urls |
| `rehype-citation` | [`@mdsvex/plugin-citations`](docs#citations) | `:cite[key]` from a CSL JSON file, and `::bibliography` |

### A component or a few lines

| plugin | now | how |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `rehype-rewrite`, `rehype-wrap`, `rehype-components` | a component | [replace the element](docs#custom-components) with a component of your own |
| `rehype-class-names`, `rehype-add-classes` | a parse plugin | `{ table: { parse(node) { node.attrs.class = 'table'; } } }` in [`parse_plugins`](docs#parse_plugins) |
| `remark-sectionize`, `rehype-section` | a parse plugin | the `sections` plugin under [plugin handlers](docs#plugin-handlers) |
| `rehype-figure`, `remark-figure-caption` | a component | `unwrap_images: true` and an `img` replacement that renders a `<figure>`, as under [`unwrap_images`](docs#unwrap_images) |
| `rehype-img-size`, `rehype-plugin-image-native-lazy-loading` | a component | an `img` replacement sets `loading="lazy"`. For sizes, import the image with `?enhanced` from [`@sveltejs/enhanced-img`][enhanced-img], the replacement gets the imported value as `src` |
| `rehype-mermaid`, `remark-mermaidjs` | a component | a [`pre` replacement](docs#code-block-components) that draws its `code` in the browser when `lang` is `mermaid` |
| `remark-embedder`, `remark-oembed`, `remark-youtube` | a component | a leaf directive, `::youtube[A talk](id=dQw4w9WgXcQ)`, see [directives](docs#the-three-forms) |
| `remark-abbr` | a component | an inline directive, `:abbr[HTML](title=HyperText)` |
| `remark-collapse` | nothing needed | `<details>` and `<summary>` work as typed, with markdown inside |
| `rehype-meta`, `rehype-document` | a template | a [template](docs#templates-1) renders `<svelte:head>` from its props |
| `rehype-video` | a component | an `a` or `img` replacement that renders a `<video>` for a video url |

### Nothing yet

| plugin | what to do today |
| --- | --- |
| `remark-github-blockquote-alert`, `remark-github-alerts`, `rehype-callouts` | `> [!NOTE]` is a block quote that starts with `[!NOTE]`. Write `:::note` with a directive component |
| `remark-heading-id`, `remark-attr`, `rehype-attr` | `{#id}` and `{.class}` can't exist, braces belong to Svelte. `heading_ids` makes ids, and a parse plugin can set any attribute |
| `remark-definition-list`, `remark-deflist` | type `<dl>`, `<dt>` and `<dd>` |
| `remark-flexible-markers`, `remark-ins` | `==mark==` and `++ins++` are text, type `<mark>` and `<ins>` |
| `remark-wiki-link` | `[[Page]]` is text |
| `remark-validate-links`, `remark-lint` | nothing checks links or style. A missing image fails the build when it is [imported](docs#import_images) |
| `rehype-sanitize` | nothing, and none is planned. A document compiles to a component and runs as code, so don't compile markdown you don't trust |
| `rehype-minify`, `rehype-format` | not needed, Svelte and Vite own the output |

A plugin that is not listed did one of three things. If it changed elements or attributes, a [parse plugin](docs#parse_plugins) can do the same. If it added syntax, a [directive](docs#directives) is the syntax mdsvex has for that, and plugins can't add more. If it needed to fetch something or read a file while compiling, a plugin does that in [deferred work](docs#deferred-work).

## Parser changes on `next`

`1.0.0-next.2` reads some documents differently from `1.0.0-next.1`. If you are already on `next`, these are the changes that can show in your pages. Each is how the parser was meant to behave, and none has an option.

### Every container holds the same blocks

A block quote, a list item, an HTML element, a Svelte block and a container directive now hold whatever the document can hold. Before, several blocks were only read at the top level and came out as text inside a container.

```svx
> | a | b |
> | --- | --- |
> | 1 | 2 |
```

```html
<!-- before -->
<blockquote><p> a  b --- --- 1  2 </p></blockquote>

<!-- after -->
<blockquote><table>…</table></blockquote>
```

```svx
- {#if show}
  text
  {/if}
```

```svelte
<!-- before -->
<li>{#if show} text {/if}</li>

<!-- after -->
<li>{#if show}<p>text</p>{/if}</li>
```

The same goes for a block quote or a directive inside a `<div>`, a list item that ends a quoted paragraph, and a link reference definition in a quote or a list item.

### A pipe outside a table is text

Every `|` in a paragraph used to be dropped.

```svx
one | two | three
```

```html
<!-- before -->
<p>one  two  three</p>

<!-- after -->
<p>one | two | three</p>
```

A `|` directly beside a `_`, `*` or `~` is still the [intraword marker](docs#different-from-commonmark) and is not rendered.

### `<pre>` and `<textarea>` hold text

Their content was parsed as markdown. Now it is kept as written, as the content of `<script>` and `<style>` is.

```svx
<pre>
line one
  indented *x*

# line three
</pre>
```

```html
<!-- before -->
<pre><p>line one indented <strong>x</strong></p><h1>line three</h1></pre>

<!-- after -->
<pre>
line one
  indented *x*

# line three
</pre>
```

Expressions and elements inside still work, since the text goes to Svelte as it is. A `<pre>` that starts a line is now a block, so it ends a paragraph or a table above it.

### A block level element at the start of a line is a block

Text after its close tag used to sit bare beside it. Now that text starts a paragraph.

```svx
<div>one</div> two
```

```html
<!-- before -->
<div>one</div> two

<!-- after -->
<div>one</div><p>two</p>
```

An inline element such as `<b>` that starts a line starts a paragraph, also when it closes on a later line. Later in a line, a block level element no longer takes the `<p>` off its paragraph, so `<Badge /> <div>a</div> text` is one paragraph.

### A NUL is text, and a stall throws

A NUL character in a document stopped the parse, and everything after it was missing. It is now a character like any other.

A parse that stops moving through its input used to log `Infinite loop detected` and return a tree cut short. It now throws a `ParseStallError` with the `offset` it stopped at, so a build fails where it used to publish half a page. If you see one, it is a bug in the parser, please report the document.

### A fence closes only at a line of backticks

A line with anything after the backticks used to close the fence and lose that text. It is now a line of code, as in CommonMark.

````svx
```md
```js
let x;
```
````

```html
<!-- before -->
<pre><code class="language-md"></code></pre><p>let x;</p><pre><code></code></pre>

<!-- after -->
<pre><code class="language-md">```js
let x;</code></pre>
```

### A backslash before another block is a backslash

A backslash at the end of a line is a hard break only when the next line carries on the paragraph. Before a blank line, the end of the document or a line that starts another block, it used to make a break, and the block after it became text.

```svx
a\
# b
```

```html
<!-- before -->
<p>a<br /> # b</p>

<!-- after -->
<p>a\</p><h1>b</h1>
```

### `[^label]` is a footnote

Footnotes are read now. A `[^1]` in the text is a reference, and a line that starts `[^1]:` is its definition. Both were plain text.

```svx
Text[^1].

[^1]: A note.
```

```html
<!-- before -->
<p>Text[^1].</p><p>[^1]: A note.</p>

<!-- after -->
<p>Text<sup><a href="#fn-1" id="fnref-1" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>.</p>
<section class="footnotes" role="doc-endnotes" aria-label="Footnotes">…</section>
```

A reference with no definition fails the build. So a `[^…]` in prose that was never a footnote, such as the regular expression class `[^abc]`, now stops the compile with a `FootnoteError`. Escape it as `\[^abc]` or put it in code. See [footnotes](docs#footnotes-1).

### Smaller changes

| you write | before | after |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `a \{1 + 1\} b` | an expression, `a 2 b` | the text `a {1 + 1} b` |
| `[a](/u\)b "t \" u")` | the backslashes stayed in the url and the title | `href="/u)b"` and `title="t &quot; u"` |
| `<div class="a" class="b">` | the first `class` was dropped | both are written, and Svelte reports the duplicate |
| an open tag over several lines, or `</a` and `>` on two lines | text | a tag |
| `<!-- note --> some text` | the comment, then a paragraph | one paragraph that holds both |
| `> _foo` then `> > bar_` | emphasis across the two quotes | `_foo`, then a quote with `bar_` |
| a code span over two lines of a block quote | the `>` of the second line was in the code | it is not |
| `- [x] done` | the text `[x] done` | a [task list](docs#task-lists) item |
| `这是*重要*的`, `“*quoted*”` | text | strong |
| `::toc`, `:::note` | text | a [directive](docs#the-three-forms), write `\::toc` for the text |
| `---` with spaces after it, around frontmatter | a thematic break | the frontmatter fence |
| `import {` over several lines | the first line only | the whole import |

## Limitations

### No indented code

Indentation means nothing in mdsvex, since it is how markup inside elements and components is laid out. Four spaces of indent don't make a code block. Use a fence.

A fence can be indented, inside an element or not. Its code keeps the indent it was written with:

````svx
<div>
	```js
	let x;
	```
</div>
````

```html
<div><pre><code class="language-js">	let x;</code></pre></div>
```

### Not read yet

- **Bare urls.** Only `<https://example.com>` is a link, unless [`@mdsvex/plugin-references`](docs#references) is set to link them.
- **GitHub alerts, definition lists, `==mark==` and wiki links**, see [nothing yet](docs#nothing-yet).

### Not possible yet

- **Replacements of its own for a component.** Markdown inside a component uses the document's replacements, see [nested scopes](docs#nested-scopes) for the way around it.
- **A plugin that waits.** Handlers are synchronous and can't read files, so a plugin can't fetch an embed or read a bibliography.
- **A plugin that adds syntax.** A plugin changes the tree the parser made, and can rewrite text, but what is markdown is fixed.
